QB.add(12,[{"id":12001,"t":"Piaget: Stage Theory","f":"in7","s":"Which list gives Piaget's four stages in order, with their approximate ages?","o":["Sensorimotor (0–2), pre-operational (2–7), concrete (7–11), formal (11+)","Pre-operational (0–2), sensorimotor (2–7), concrete (7–11), formal (11+)","Sensorimotor (0–2), concrete (2–7), pre-operational (7–11), formal (11+)","Sensorimotor (0–2), pre-operational (2–7), formal (7–11), concrete operational (11+)","Sensorimotor (0–5), pre-operational (5–9), concrete (9–13), formal (13+)"],"e":"Piaget's stages: <b>sensorimotor (0–2)</b> – thinking by doing; <b>pre-operational (2–7)</b> – symbols, egocentric, no conservation; <b>concrete operational (7–~11)</b> – logical but not abstract; <b>formal operational (~11+)</b> – abstract, hypothetical reasoning. The <b>order is universal</b>, though the <b>age may vary</b>. Memory aid: Silly Pigs Can Fly.","w":["Sensorimotor is first.","Pre-operational is before concrete.","Concrete comes before formal.","Ages are wrong."],"k":"Piaget: sensorimotor → pre-op → concrete → formal.","rn":[["","What are Piaget's 4 stages of intellectual development (in order)?","What are Piaget's 4 stages of intellectual development (in order)?","Sensorimotor; Pre-operational; Concrete operational; Formal operational; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":12002,"t":"Piaget: Stage Theory","s":"According to Piaget, which statement about stages is correct?","o":["The order is universal, but ages may vary","The order varies by culture, but ages are fixed","The order and ages are both universal and fixed","Stages can be skipped if a child is taught well","Children can be in several stages at once, by design"],"e":"Piaget: mental development proceeds through <b>4 key stages</b>; the <b>order is universal</b> (regardless of culture or experience), but the <b>age may vary</b>. Fluid, overlapping stages are Vygotsky's view.","w":["Order is universal.","Ages vary.","No skipping.","Discrete stages (Piaget)."],"k":"Piaget: universal order, variable ages.","rn":[["","What is universal in Piaget's stages?","What is universal in Piaget's stages?","The order of the stages"]]},{"id":12003,"t":"Piaget: Stage Theory","s":"An 8-month-old searches for a toy after it is hidden under a blanket. Name the ability shown.","e":"Object permanence (sensorimotor stage, 0–2): knowing objects still exist when hidden.","k":"Object permanence.","a":["object permanence","object constancy"],"rn":[["","Object Permanence","Object Permanence","Things can exist if not visible; Learnt in the sensorimotor stage"]]},{"id":12004,"t":"Piaget: Stage Theory","s":"A baby in a high chair sweeps a bowl onto the floor and the dog rushes over excitedly; the baby repeats it. Which sensorimotor learning does this illustrate?","o":["Their actions cause things to happen and others react","Objects can change shape but keep the same volume","Actions can be reversed to return to the start","Other people have different beliefs and desires from their own","Symbols can stand for real objects in pretend play"],"e":"In the sensorimotor stage the infant learns that <b>their actions cause things to happen</b> and make others react – the lecturer's high-chair example.","w":["Conservation (concrete).","Reversibility (concrete).","Theory of mind.","Pre-operational."],"k":"Sensorimotor: actions cause effects.","rn":[["","What do children learn about their actions in the sensorimotor stage?","What do children learn about their actions in the sensorimotor stage?","Actions cause things to happen; Other people react"]]},{"id":12005,"t":"Piaget: Stage Theory","s":"A 4-year-old pretends to “have a cup of tea” from an empty cup but refuses to share a toy, saying “No, it's mine!”. Which stage features do these show?","o":["Symbolic thinking and egocentrism – pre-operational","Symbolic thinking and conservation – concrete operational","Object permanence and egocentrism – sensorimotor","Abstract reasoning and egocentrism – formal operational","Classification and reversibility – concrete operational"],"e":"<b>Pre-operational</b> (2–7): <b>symbolic thinking</b> shown in pretend play, and <b>egocentrism</b> (struggles with others' perspectives – why sharing is hard). This starts to change late in the stage.","w":["No conservation yet.","Pretend play is pre-operational.","Not abstract.","Not shown."],"k":"Pretend play + egocentrism = pre-operational.","rn":[["","In which stage does symbolic thinking begin?","In which stage does symbolic thinking begin?","Pre-operational"],["","What are the 5 features of the pre-operational stage?","What are the 5 features of the pre-operational stage?","Emergence of language; Beginnings of symbolic thinking; Egocentricity; Concrete thinking; Lack of concept of conservation"]]},{"id":12006,"t":"Piaget: Stage Theory","s":"A child watches juice poured from a short wide glass into a tall thin one and says there is now “more”. Name the concept the child lacks.","e":"Conservation (quantity stays the same despite a change in shape) is lacking in the pre-operational stage (2–7) and acquired in the concrete operational stage.","k":"Conservation.","a":["conservation","conservation of volume","conservation of quantity"],"rn":[["","What are the 5 features of the pre-operational stage?","What are the 5 features of the pre-operational stage?","Emergence of language; Beginnings of symbolic thinking; Egocentricity; Concrete thinking; Lack of concept of conservation"]]},{"id":12007,"t":"Piaget: Stage Theory","s":"A child understands that 2 + 4 = 6 and therefore 6 − 2 = 4, and has started collecting particular toys. Which stage is most likely?","o":["Concrete operational","Pre-operational","Formal operational","Sensorimotor","Psychophysiological"],"e":"<b>Concrete operational</b> (7–~11): <b>reversibility</b> (2 + 4 = 6 → 6 − 2 = 4), conservation of number, <b>classification</b> (the age of collecting obsessions), organised thinking and inductive logic.","w":["No reversibility yet.","Abstract reasoning.","Thinking by doing.","Bibace and Walsh stage."],"k":"Reversibility + classification = concrete operational.","rn":[["","What are the 7 features of the concrete operational stage?","What are the 7 features of the concrete operational stage?","Experiment with and manipulate real objects; Conservation of number, reversibility; Classification; organised thinking; Inductive logic; Still struggle with abstract/hypothetical; Less egocentric; Thoughts are unique"],["","What 2 things about number are gained in the concrete operational stage?","What 2 things about number are gained in the concrete operational stage?","Conservation of number; Reversibility"],["","What kind of thinking appears in the concrete operational stage?","What kind of thinking appears in the concrete operational stage?","Organised thinking; Classification of objects"]]},{"id":12008,"t":"Piaget: Stage Theory","s":"Which ability is still difficult in the concrete operational stage?","o":["Abstract, hypothetical concepts","Conservation of number","Classification of objects into groups","Reversibility of operations","Organised, logical thinking"],"e":"Concrete operational children still struggle with <b>abstract and hypothetical</b> concepts (still concrete; <b>magical thinking</b>). They can conserve, reverse, classify and are less egocentric.","w":["Achieved.","Achieved.","Achieved.","Achieved."],"k":"Concrete stage: abstract thought still hard.","rn":[["","What do concrete operational children still struggle with?","What do concrete operational children still struggle with?","Abstract and hypothetical concepts"]]},{"id":12009,"t":"Piaget: Stage Theory","s":"Asked how someone who stole a loaf of bread should be punished, one child says “jail, they're bad”; another considers whether they were feeding their family. Which stage does the second child show?","o":["Formal operational – abstract reasoning about circumstances","Concrete operational – logical reasoning about real objects","Pre-operational – egocentric reasoning about rules","Formal operational – egocentric reasoning about rules","Concrete operational – abstract reasoning about circumstances"],"e":"The older child (11+) shows <b>formal operational</b> thinking: complex <b>abstract reasoning</b> about hypothetical questions, considering <b>circumstances</b> and a <b>range of possible outcomes</b>, including moral and social issues.","w":["Abstract.","Not egocentric.","Not egocentric.","Abstract is formal."],"k":"Loaf of bread: formal operational reasoning.","rn":[["","What are the 4 features of the formal operational stage?","What are the 4 features of the formal operational stage?","Complex abstract reasoning about hypotheticals; Moral, philosophical, ethical, social, political issues; Deductive logic; Range of possible outcomes"],["","Formal operational stage: what develops?","Formal operational stage: what develops?","Abstract thinking"]]},{"id":12010,"t":"Piaget: Stage Theory","s":"What is the difference between inductive and deductive logic, as linked to Piaget's stages?","o":["Inductive: specific → general (concrete); deductive: general → specific (formal)","Inductive: general → specific (concrete); deductive: specific → general (formal)","Inductive: specific → general (formal); deductive: general → specific (concrete)","Inductive and deductive both appear in the pre-operational stage","Inductive: specific → general (sensorimotor); deductive: general → specific (concrete)"],"e":"<b>Inductive</b> = specific → general (<b>concrete operational</b>). <b>Deductive</b> = general → specific (<b>formal operational</b>).","w":["Definitions swapped.","Stages swapped.","Not stated.","Stages wrong."],"k":"Inductive (concrete) vs deductive (formal).","rn":[["","Inductive vs deductive logic: which Piaget stage?","Inductive vs deductive logic: which Piaget stage?","Inductive: concrete operational; Deductive: formal operational"],["","What type of logic is used in the concrete operational stage?","What type of logic is used in the concrete operational stage?","Inductive logic; From specific information and general principles"]]},{"id":12011,"t":"Piaget: Stage Theory","s":"Which statement about Piaget's view of the child's mind is correct?","o":["Not a miniature adult mind; learning through action","A miniature adult mind; learning through action","Not a miniature adult mind; learning through passive listening","A miniature adult mind; learning through social mentors","Not a miniature adult mind; learning set by culture"],"e":"Piaget emphasised <b>action and problem solving</b> in learning, and held that the <b>child's mind is not a miniature version of an adult's</b>.","w":["Not miniature.","Action and problem solving.","Vygotsky.","Vygotsky."],"k":"Child's mind ≠ mini adult.","rn":[["","What did Piaget say about the child's mind?","What did Piaget say about the child's mind?","Not a miniature version of an adult's mind; Children are cognitively different"]]},{"id":12012,"t":"Vygotsky: Sociocultural Theory","s":"Which statement best summarises Vygotsky's sociocultural theory?","o":["A social process shaped by mentors, peers and culture; fluid stages","A social process shaped by mentors and peers; universal discrete stages","An individual process of exploration; fluid stages","An individual process of exploration; universal discrete stages","A biological process, little affected by culture; fluid stages"],"e":"Vygotsky (1896–1934): human learning is a <b>social process</b>; <b>mentors and peers</b> influence learning; <b>cultural beliefs</b> affect learning; each culture provides <b>“tools of intellectual adaptation”</b> (music, story, rote learning); stages are <b>fluid</b>.","w":["Fluid.","Social.","Piaget.","Social/cultural."],"k":"Vygotsky: social, cultural, fluid.","rn":[["","What are the 5 points of Vygotsky's Sociocultural Theory?","What are the 5 points of Vygotsky's Sociocultural Theory?","Learning is a social process; Mentors and peers influence learning; Cultural beliefs affect learning; Learning from others integrated individually; Culture provides 'tools of intellectual adaptation'"]]},{"id":12013,"t":"Vygotsky: Sociocultural Theory","s":"A child cannot cut an apple alone but can with a parent's guidance. Name Vygotsky's term for this gap.","e":"The zone of proximal development is the gap between what a child can do alone and with help.","k":"ZPD.","a":["zone of proximal development","zpd","proximal development"],"rn":[["","ZPD","ZPD","Zone of proximal development"]]},{"id":12014,"t":"Vygotsky: Sociocultural Theory","s":"A parent guides a child's hands on a knife and gradually withdraws help as the child improves. Name Vygotsky's term for this support.","e":"Scaffolding: supported learning that builds on existing skills, withdrawn as competence grows.","k":"Scaffolding.","a":["scaffolding","scaffold"],"rn":[["","Scaffolding","Scaffolding","Learning is supported; Existing skills and knowledge instil confidence to progress"],["","What 3 concepts come from Vygotsky's theory?","What 3 concepts come from Vygotsky's theory?","Zone of proximal development; Scaffolding; Language"]]},{"id":12015,"t":"Vygotsky: Sociocultural Theory","s":"A 4-year-old talks aloud to herself while doing a puzzle. In Vygotsky's account, what does this speech later become?","e":"Egocentric speech becomes “inner speech”.","k":"Inner speech.","a":["inner speech","internal speech","inner voice","thought"],"rn":[["","Egocentric Speech","Egocentric Speech","Self-talking out loud; Eventually becomes 'inner speech'"],["","What are the 6 key features of Vygotsky's theory?","What are the 6 key features of Vygotsky's theory?","Stages are fluid; Importance of play; Reasoning limited at any age; Full development needs social interaction; Shared problem-solving; Egocentric speech becomes inner speech"]]},{"id":12016,"t":"Vygotsky: Sociocultural Theory","s":"Which statement about Vygotsky's key features is correct?","o":["Play matters, shared problem-solving helps, culture affects development","Play matters, solo exploration helps most, and culture affects development","Play matters, shared problem-solving helps, stages are universal","Play matters little; shared problem-solving helps most","Play matters; development is identical across cultures"],"e":"Key features: stages are <b>fluid</b>; <b>play</b> is important; reasoning is limited at any age; full development <b>requires social interaction</b> and is <b>culturally dependent</b>; children learn best through <b>problem-solving shared with someone else</b>.","w":["Shared.","Fluid stages.","Play is important.","Cultural."],"k":"Vygotsky key features.","rn":[["","What are the 6 key features of Vygotsky's theory?","What are the 6 key features of Vygotsky's theory?","Stages are fluid; Importance of play; Reasoning limited at any age; Full development needs social interaction; Shared problem-solving; Egocentric speech becomes inner speech"]]},{"id":12017,"t":"Vygotsky: Sociocultural Theory","s":"Which comparison of Piaget and Vygotsky is correct?","o":["Piaget: own exploration, universal stages; Vygotsky: social, fluid, cultural","Piaget: social, fluid, cultural; Vygotsky: own exploration, universal stages","Piaget: own exploration, fluid stages; Vygotsky: social, universal stages","Both: own exploration, universal stages, little role for culture","Both: social factors, fluid stages, culture very important"],"e":"<b>Piaget</b>: the child's own <b>interaction and exploration</b>; <b>discrete, universal</b> stages; little role for culture; more “biological”. <b>Vygotsky</b>: <b>social factors</b> (mentors, peers, culture); <b>fluid</b> stages; development <b>differs between cultures</b>; more “socio-cultural”.","w":["Swapped.","Stage type swapped.","Vygotsky is social.","Piaget less social."],"k":"Piaget vs Vygotsky table.","rn":[["","Vygotsky's stages vs Piaget's stages: how fixed?","Vygotsky's stages vs Piaget's stages: how fixed?","Vygotsky: fluid, less fixed; Piaget: discrete, universal"],["","Piaget vs Vygotsky: development across cultures?","Piaget vs Vygotsky: development across cultures?","Piaget: stages discrete and universal; Vygotsky: differs between cultures"],["","Piaget vs Vygotsky: what influences development?","Piaget vs Vygotsky: what influences development?","Piaget: interaction and exploration of the environment; Vygotsky: social factors"]]},{"id":12018,"t":"Vygotsky: Sociocultural Theory","s":"Which view on Piaget versus Vygotsky is best supported?","o":["Both matter: broadly universal stages, but social input is vital","Piaget was right: stages are universal and culture adds little","Vygotsky was right: stages are cultural and biology adds little","Neither: development is mainly set by genetics","Both were wrong about stages, which do not exist"],"e":"Today stages are recognised as <b>broadly universal</b>, but parents, peers and culture are <b>very important</b> (isolated or abused children fall behind). Piaget was perhaps <b>too focused on biology</b>; Vygotsky perhaps <b>ignored biology and evolution</b>. It is <b>nature by nurture</b>.","w":["Nature by nurture.","Nature by nurture.","Not stated.","Stages recognised."],"k":"Both theories matter; nature by nurture.","rn":[["","What is recognised today about the two theories?","What is recognised today about the two theories?","Stages are universal; But parents, peers and culture are very important; Both matter"]]},{"id":12019,"t":"Bowlby: Attachment Theory","s":"According to Bowlby (1969), what is the primary evolutionary function of attachment bonds?","o":["Safety and essential care","Learning language from caregivers","Forming peer groups for later life","Developing abstract reasoning","Promoting independence from caregivers"],"e":"<b>Bowlby (1969)</b>: the primary (evolutionary) function of attachment bonds is <b>safety</b> and the giving and receiving of <b>essential care</b>.","w":["Not stated.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Attachment: safety and care.","rn":[["","What is the primary (evolutionary) function of attachment bonds?","What is the primary (evolutionary) function of attachment bonds?","Safety; Giving and receiving of essential care"]]},{"id":12020,"t":"Bowlby: Attachment Theory","s":"Which developmental abilities does secure attachment support?","o":["Socialising, communication, emotional regulation, health and sense of self","Socialising, communication and motor skills, but not emotional regulation or sense of self","Physical health and growth only","Reading and arithmetic only","Emotional regulation only"],"e":"<b>Secure attachment</b> is vital to <b>socialising, speech and communication, emotional regulation, physical health</b>, and a <b>sense of self and other people</b> (mentalising and empathising). It provides a <b>secure base</b> to explore from (Howe, 2011).","w":["Emotional regulation too.","More.","Not stated.","More."],"k":"Secure attachment supports many abilities.","rn":[["","What 5 abilities does secure attachment support?","What 5 abilities does secure attachment support?","Socialising; Speech and communication; Emotional regulation; Physical health; Sense of self and other people"]]},{"id":12021,"t":"Bowlby: Attachment Theory","s":"A toddler in a new clinic playroom explores the toys but keeps returning to her mother before venturing out again. Name Bowlby's term for the role the mother plays.","e":"Bowlby (1969): attachment bonds evolved for safety and essential care. A securely attached child uses the caregiver as a secure base from which to explore, supporting socialising, communication, emotional regulation, health and sense of self.","k":"Secure base.","a":["secure base","a secure base","safe base"],"rn":[["","What does a secure attachment provide?","What does a secure attachment provide?","A 'Secure Base'"],["","What does the child do from the 'Secure Base'?","What does the child do from the 'Secure Base'?","Begins to explore the world"]]},{"id":12022,"t":"Theory of Mind","s":"Name the ability, probed by the Sally–Anne test, to attribute beliefs and feelings to others.","e":"Theory of mind emerges around 3–5 years; Baron-Cohen et al. (1985) found only 4 of 20 autistic children passed the Sally–Anne task.","k":"Theory of mind.","a":["theory of mind","tom","mentalising","mentalizing"],"rn":[["","What is the most famous test of theory of mind?","What is the most famous test of theory of mind?","The Sally-Anne test"],["","Image card: What test is this? Where will Sally look, and what does that show?","What test is this? Where will Sally look, and what does that show?","Sally-Anne test (theory of mind); In the basket; Knows others can hold different beliefs"],["","Sally-Anne test: answer of a child with theory of mind?","Sally-Anne test: answer of a child with theory of mind?","The basket"]]},{"id":12023,"t":"Theory of Mind","f":"in17","s":"In the Sally–Anne test, Sally puts her ball in her basket and leaves; Anne moves it to her box. A child says Sally will look “in the box”. What does this show?","o":["The child lacks theory of mind","The child has theory of mind","The child lacks object permanence","The child lacks conservation","The child lacks secure attachment"],"e":"Answering <b>“in the box”</b> (where it really is) shows <b>no theory of mind</b> yet: the child assumes Sally knows what <i>they</i> know. “In the basket” shows understanding of Sally's <b>false belief</b>.","w":["Not yet.","They know where it is.","Not tested.","Not tested."],"k":"Sally–Anne: “box” = no ToM; “basket” = ToM.","rn":[["","Sally-Anne test: answer of a child with theory of mind?","Sally-Anne test: answer of a child with theory of mind?","The basket"],["","Sally-Anne test: answer of a child without theory of mind?","Sally-Anne test: answer of a child without theory of mind?","The box"]]},{"id":12024,"t":"Theory of Mind","s":"At what age does theory of mind typically emerge?","o":["Around 3–5 years","Around 0–6 months","Around 11–12 years","Around 16 years","Only in adulthood"],"e":"Theory of mind <b>emerges around 3–5 years</b>, and continues to develop through childhood and adolescence.","w":["Too early.","Too late.","Too late.","Too late."],"k":"ToM emerges ~3–5 y.","rn":[["","When does theory of mind start to emerge?","When does theory of mind start to emerge?","3 to 5 years of age"]]},{"id":12025,"t":"Theory of Mind","s":"What did Baron-Cohen et al. (1985) find when testing autistic children on the Sally–Anne task?","o":["Only 4 of 20 answered correctly","Only 16 of 20 answered correctly","All 20 answered correctly","Only 4 of 20 answered incorrectly","Only 10 of 20 answered correctly"],"e":"<b>Simon Baron-Cohen</b> found only <b>4 out of 20 autistic children</b> answered the theory-of-mind question correctly. Impairments may be signs of <b>neurodiversity</b> or developmental delay.","w":["4.","4.","Reversed.","4."],"k":"Baron-Cohen: 4/20 autistic children correct.","rn":[["","Sally-Anne test: answer of a child with theory of mind?","Sally-Anne test: answer of a child with theory of mind?","The basket"],["","Sally-Anne test: answer of a child without theory of mind?","Sally-Anne test: answer of a child without theory of mind?","The box"]]},{"id":12026,"t":"Theory of Mind","s":"Why is theory of mind relevant in consultations with children under 7?","o":["They may assume the doctor already knows how they feel","They may describe their pain precisely using adult medical terms","They may refuse to speak to the doctor at all","They may understand stress as a cause of illness","They may not feel pain until they are older"],"e":"Under-7s may struggle to understand their own sensations and describe them to someone who doesn't know how they feel; a young child may assume the doctor <b>already knows</b>. ToM development is <b>active</b>, so children can learn to communicate feelings more accurately (“Dr can't know your tummy hurts, so let's explore it together”).","w":["Under-7s struggle.","Not stated.","Psychophysiological is 16+.","Not stated."],"k":"ToM: child may assume doctor already knows.","rn":[["","Why may a young child not describe symptoms well?","Why may a young child not describe symptoms well?","Assumes the doctor already knows how they feel"]]},{"id":12027,"t":"Theory of Mind","s":"Which statement about the development of theory of mind is correct?","o":["It keeps developing and can be learned","It is fixed by age 3 and cannot be learned","It is fully developed at age 7 and fixed after that","It develops only in children without autism","It is lost in adolescence and regained later"],"e":"Theory of mind <b>continues to develop</b> through childhood and adolescence, becoming more sophisticated with age and experience; it is an <b>active process</b>.","w":["Keeps developing.","Continues into adolescence.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"ToM develops and can be learned.","rn":[["","What can children learn as theory of mind develops?","What can children learn as theory of mind develops?","Communicate their feelings more accurately"]]},{"id":12028,"t":"Children's Understanding of Illness","s":"A 3-year-old says “that food made me ill” but has no idea how. Name the Bibace and Walsh stage.","e":"Phenomenism: illness linked to an external event with no idea of mechanism. Order: phenomenism, contagion, contamination, internalisation, physiological, psychophysiological.","k":"Phenomenism.","a":["phenomenism","phenomenalism"],"rn":[["","What are the 6 Bibace & Walsh stages of explaining illness (in order)?","What are the 6 Bibace & Walsh stages of explaining illness (in order)?","Phenomenism (2-4); Contagion (4-7); Contamination (7-9); Internalisation (9-11); Physiological (11-16); Psychophysiological (16+); MNEMONIC"],["","Which stage: \"the food made me ill, I won't eat it\"?","Which stage: \"the food made me ill, I won't eat it\"?","Phenomenism"]]},{"id":12029,"t":"Children's Understanding of Illness","s":"A 6-year-old says “I caught a cold from my friend because I sat next to her.” Which stage is this?","o":["Contagion","Phenomenism","Contamination","Physiological","Psychophysiological"],"e":"<b>Contagion (4–7)</b>: illness caused by <b>proximity</b> to ill people or objects.","w":["No mechanism.","Physical contact; punishment.","Organ malfunction.","Stress affects body."],"k":"Contagion: proximity.","rn":[["","Contagion","Contagion","Age 4-7; Illness caused by proximity to ill people or particular objects"]]},{"id":12030,"t":"Children's Understanding of Illness","s":"An 8-year-old says “I did something naughty so I got sick.” Which stage best fits?","o":["Contamination","Contagion","Internalisation","Phenomenism","Psychophysiological"],"e":"<b>Contamination (7–9)</b>: illness from <b>physical contact</b> with an ill person, and may be seen as <b>punishment for misbehaviour</b>.","w":["Proximity.","Inside the body.","No mechanism.","Stress."],"k":"Contamination: contact; punishment.","rn":[["","Contamination","Contamination","Age 7-9; Illness caused by physical contact with an ill person; May be seen as punishment for misbehaviour"]]},{"id":12031,"t":"Children's Understanding of Illness","s":"A 10-year-old explains “I caught a cold because I was cold.” Which stage is this?","o":["Internalisation","Contamination","Physiological","Contagion","Phenomenism"],"e":"<b>Internalisation (9–11)</b>: illness is <b>inside the body</b>, but may be caused by <b>external factors</b> (“because I was cold”).","w":["Physical contact.","Organ function.","Proximity.","Magical."],"k":"Internalisation: inside body, external cause.","rn":[["","Internalisation","Internalisation","Age 9-11; Illness located within the body; But may be caused by external factors"],["","Which stage: \"I was cold, therefore I caught a cold\"?","Which stage: \"I was cold, therefore I caught a cold\"?","Internalisation"],["","Give an example of an internalisation explanation of illness.","Give an example of an internalisation explanation of illness.","Catching a cold because you are cold"]]},{"id":12032,"t":"Children's Understanding of Illness","s":"A 17-year-old with asthma says exam stress triggered her attacks. Name the Bibace and Walsh stage.","e":"Psychophysiological: understanding that psychological factors (stress, worry, sleep) affect bodily illness; the final stage.","k":"Psychophysiological.","a":["psychophysiological","psycho-physiological","psychophysiologic"],"rn":[["","Which psychological and lifestyle factors feature in psychophysiological explanations o…","Which psychological and lifestyle factors feature in psychophysiological explanations of illness?","Stress; Fatigue; Alcohol use; Drug use"],["","Psychophysiological","Psychophysiological","Age 16+; Stress and fatigue can affect physiological processes; Rather than just being an outcome"],["","What are the 6 Bibace & Walsh stages of explaining illness (in order)?","What are the 6 Bibace & Walsh stages of explaining illness (in order)?","Phenomenism (2-4); Contagion (4-7); Contamination (7-9); Internalisation (9-11); Physiological (11-16); Psychophysiological (16+); MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":12033,"t":"Children's Understanding of Illness","s":"Which list gives the Bibace and Walsh stages in order?","o":["Phenomenism, contagion, contamination, internalisation, physiological, psychophysiological","Contagion, phenomenism, contamination, internalisation, physiological, psychophysiological","Phenomenism, contamination, contagion, internalisation, physiological, psychophysiological","Phenomenism, contagion, contamination, physiological, internalisation, psychophysiological","Phenomenism, contagion, internalisation, contamination, physiological, psychophysiological"],"e":"Order: <b>phenomenism → contagion → contamination → internalisation → physiological → psychophysiological</b> (memory aid: Please Can Cats Inspect Purple Pyjamas?).","w":["Phenomenism first.","Contagion before contamination.","Internalisation before physiological.","Contamination before internalisation."],"k":"Bibace and Walsh order.","rn":[["","What are the 6 Bibace & Walsh stages of explaining illness (in order)?","What are the 6 Bibace & Walsh stages of explaining illness (in order)?","Phenomenism (2-4); Contagion (4-7); Contamination (7-9); Internalisation (9-11); Physiological (11-16); Psychophysiological (16+); MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":12034,"t":"Piaget: Stage Theory","s":"Why is it hard to explain to a 7–9-year-old that poor sleep or exam worry could make them ill?","o":["They are not yet at the psychophysiological stage","They have not yet developed theory of mind","They are still in the sensorimotor stage","They are already at the physiological stage","They lack object permanence"],"e":"At 7–9 children are typically in the <b>contamination</b> stage; understanding that psychological factors affect the body (<b>psychophysiological</b>) comes at 16+, so there is a <b>disconnect</b>.","w":["Not the reason.","Not.","Not until 11+.","Not relevant."],"k":"Psychophysiological understanding comes late.","rn":[["","Concrete operational stage: what happens to thinking?","Concrete operational stage: what happens to thinking?","Becomes logical; But not yet abstract"]]},{"id":12035,"t":"Children's Understanding of Illness","s":"A 7-year-old with diabetes has had stories, a diabetes nurse and a support group, but cannot link feeling unwell to her diabetes. How should this shape your approach?","o":["Tailor explanations to her stage, while acknowledging her experience","Explain the full physiology of insulin, since she has had support group input","Assume she understands, given her stories and support group","Speak only to her parents about her diabetes","Avoid discussing diabetes until she is older"],"e":"The example shows that even with support a 7-year-old may understand only that diabetes is “special”, needs medicine and affects food. Make explanations <b>match age and experience</b>, use creative age-appropriate means, and check readiness.","w":["Too abstract.","She may not.","Talk to her.","Talk to her."],"k":"Tailor explanations to stage and experience.","rn":[["","What are the 5 points of Step 2?","What are the 5 points of Step 2?","Assess developmental stage; Match explanation to age and experience; Creative, age-appropriate communication; Consider language and concepts used; Attention to the child in the moment"]]},{"id":12036,"t":"Children's Understanding of Illness","s":"Which factors affect a child's understanding of illness?","o":["Age/stage, language, culture, experience and family","Age and stage, language, culture and parental income only","Age only, since stages are fixed","Personal experience and school grades only","Culture and family factors, but not age"],"e":"Influences: <b>age and stage</b>, <b>language/communication</b>, <b>cultural background</b>, <b>personal experience</b> (long-standing conditions, siblings) and <b>systemic/family factors</b> (e.g. disability and medicalised environments).","w":["More factors.","More.","More.","Age matters."],"k":"Influences on illness understanding.","rn":[["","What 5 factors does children's understanding of illness depend on?","What 5 factors does children's understanding of illness depend on?","Age and stage of development; Language / communication style and ability; Cultural background; Personal experience; Systemic and family factors"]]},{"id":12037,"t":"Children's Understanding of Illness","s":"A child with a long-standing condition knows far more about it than expected for their age. What does this illustrate?","o":["Personal experience shapes understanding","The child must be older than their stated age","Bibace and Walsh stages always predict exactly","Experience has little effect compared with the child’s age","The child is likely to be gifted academically"],"e":"<b>Personal experience</b> – long-standing conditions or siblings with them – can mean a child knows much more than expected; acknowledge <b>wider experience</b>, not just age.","w":["Not stated.","Not exact.","It has.","Not stated."],"k":"Experience can outpace age.","rn":[["","What 5 factors does children's understanding of illness depend on?","What 5 factors does children's understanding of illness depend on?","Age and stage of development; Language / communication style and ability; Cultural background; Personal experience; Systemic and family factors"]]},{"id":12038,"t":"Children's Understanding of Illness","s":"A 6-year-old needs a cannula tomorrow. Her parents ask how best to prepare her. Which approach is most appropriate?","o":["An easy-read picture book and a play therapist demonstrating on a doll","A detailed written leaflet for her to read alone the night before","A full explanation of venous anatomy and how the cannula works, given beforehand","Telling her nothing, so that she does not become anxious","Asking her to rate the expected pain on a numerical scale first"],"e":"Explanations should match the child's age, stage and experience. Useful resources: easy reads, pictures, websites, nurses and play therapy (e.g. a teddy or doll to show a procedure).","w":["Written adult-style leaflets do not match her developmental stage.","Abstract physiology is beyond a child of this age.","Direct, age-appropriate communication helps; avoidance does not.","Numerical scales suit older children; this is not preparation."],"k":"Creative, age-appropriate preparation.","rn":[["","What 3 resources help explain illness to children?","What 3 resources help explain illness to children?","Easy reads; Picture resources; Websites"]]},{"id":12039,"t":"Pain and Healthcare in Childhood","s":"A 7-year-old has fractured her arm. What is the gold-standard way to measure her pain?","e":"Self-report (asking the child) is the gold standard; face scales suit young children, numerical/VAS scales older ones.","k":"Self-report.","a":["self-report","self report","ask the child","asking the child","child self-report"],"rn":[["","Why is self-report the gold standard for pain?","Why is self-report the gold standard for pain?","Pain is inherently subjective"],["","How do you measure a child's pain?","How do you measure a child's pain?","Ask them! (self-report)"]]},{"id":12040,"t":"Pain and Healthcare in Childhood","f":"in26","s":"Which tool is appropriate for measuring pain in a young child, and what is a limitation of such scales?","o":["A face pain scale (FACES); scales are prone to bias and anchors","A numerical scale; scales have no biases","A visual analogue scale; scales are free of past experience","A face pain scale (FACES); scales have no limitations","A numerical scale for infants; scale anchors do not matter"],"e":"<b>Face pain scales</b> (e.g. <b>FACES</b>, Wong and Baker 1988) suit younger children; <b>numerical or visual analogue</b> scales suit older children. All are subject to <b>biases, past experience and scale anchors</b> (words at each end).","w":["Younger child.","Biases.","Limitations exist.","Older children."],"k":"FACES for young children; scale biases.","rn":[["","What 3 things can bias pain scales?","What 3 things can bias pain scales?","Biases; Past experience; Scale anchors"]]},{"id":12041,"t":"Pain and Healthcare in Childhood","s":"From what age does basic pain vocabulary (“ow”, “boo-boo”) emerge?","o":["As early as 18 months","As early as 6 months","At about 3 years","At about 5 years","At about 7 years"],"e":"Basic pain vocabulary emerges as early as <b>18 months</b> (Stanford et al., 2005).","w":["18 months.","18 months.","18 months.","18 months."],"k":"Pain words from ~18 months.","rn":[["","When does basic pain vocabulary emerge?","When does basic pain vocabulary emerge?","As early as 18 months"]]},{"id":12042,"t":"Pain and Healthcare in Childhood","s":"Which factors shape children's experience of pain (Kortesluoma and Nikkonen, 2004)?","o":["Context, fear, parents’ reactions, “being brave”","Context and fear of procedures, but not parents’ reactions","Tissue damage alone, independent of context","Age and sex alone","Parents’ reactions only"],"e":"Pain experience is <b>context dependent</b>; involves <b>fear and anticipation</b> from previous procedures; <b>systemic fears</b> (how parents react); and <b>social concepts</b> of fighting illness and being brave, which can sometimes reduce pain.","w":["Parents too.","Context dependent.","Not stated.","More."],"k":"Influences on children's pain.","rn":[["","What are the 4 factors in the experience of pain in childhood?","What are the 4 factors in the experience of pain in childhood?","Context dependent; Fear and anticipation; Systemic fears; Social concepts of 'fighting' illness"]]},{"id":12043,"t":"Pain and Healthcare in Childhood","s":"Which statement about children's experience of healthcare is correct?","o":["Experiences are mediated by adults; direct communication helps","Experiences are mediated by adults; direct communication should be avoided","Experiences are independent of adults; direct communication helps","Anxious adults tend to calm the child","Young children focus on long-term treatment plans"],"e":"Experiences are <b>mediated by adults</b> (anxious adults → anxious child). <b>Direct communication</b> improves <b>trust, compliance, outcomes and satisfaction</b>. Young children focus on <b>immediate concerns</b>; involvement preferences vary.","w":["Direct helps.","Mediated.","Anxious → anxious.","Immediate concerns."],"k":"Direct communication improves outcomes.","rn":[["","What are the 5 points on children's experience of healthcare and doctors?","What are the 5 points on children's experience of healthcare and doctors?","Mediated by adults around them; Direct child-clinician communication helps; Context of child's life; Immediate concerns in young children; How involved does the child want to be?"]]},{"id":12044,"t":"Pain and Healthcare in Childhood","s":"A young child's main question after a diagnosis is “Can I still go to the birthday party?” What does this reflect?","o":["Young children focus on immediate concerns","Young children focus on long-term outcomes","Young children reason formally about illness","Young children understand psychophysiology","Young children want every detail of treatment"],"e":"Young children focus on <b>immediate concerns</b> (“Can I still go to the birthday party?”).","w":["Immediate.","Not formal.","16+.","Not stated."],"k":"Children focus on the immediate.","rn":[["","What are the 5 points on children's experience of healthcare and doctors?","What are the 5 points on children's experience of healthcare and doctors?","Mediated by adults around them; Direct child-clinician communication helps; Context of child's life; Immediate concerns in young children; How involved does the child want to be?"]]},{"id":12045,"t":"Health Risks in Adolescence","s":"Why does adolescence matter for lifelong health?","o":["Adolescents build resources that underpin later health and wellbeing","Adolescents build resources that matter mainly during adolescence itself, not later","Adolescence is mostly physical change with little long-term effect","Adolescence ends by about 13 for most people","Adolescence is the same in all primate species"],"e":"During adolescence, an individual acquires <b>cognitive, emotional, physical and social resources</b> that become the <b>foundation for later life, health and wellbeing</b>.","w":["Foundation for later life.","Not only physical.","Not stated.","Only Homo sapiens."],"k":"Adolescence builds lifelong foundations.","rn":[["","Give 3 examples of adolescent health risks.","Give 3 examples of adolescent health risks.","Eating disorders; Schizophrenia; Overweight"]]},{"id":12046,"t":"Part 2: Growing Up and Adolescence","f":"in31","s":"What does the life-history chart of hominins show about adolescence?","o":["Only Homo sapiens has a distinct adolescence","All hominins have a distinct adolescence","Australopithecus had the longest adolescence","Humans lack a juvenile stage","Most mammals have a distinct adolescence"],"e":"The chart shows an extra <b>adolescence</b> stage appearing in <b>Homo sapiens</b> but not earlier species such as <i>Australopithecus afarensis</i>. Most animals go infant → juvenile → adult.","w":["Only sapiens.","Not shown.","They have one.","Infant → juvenile → adult."],"k":"Adolescence is distinctly human.","rn":[["","Image card: Which species has an adolescence stage here? What else is longer?","Which species has an adolescence stage here? What else is longer?","Only H sapiens; Extended childhood"]]},{"id":12047,"t":"Puberty and Pubertal Timing","s":"Which pairing of pubertal event and description is correct?","o":["Gonadarche – HPG activation ending in reproductive competence","Adrenarche – HPG activation ending in reproductive competence","Gonadarche – HPA activation releasing adrenal androgens","Adrenarche – HPA activation ending in reproductive competence","Gonadarche – HPG activation, usually before adrenarche"],"e":"<b>Gonadarche</b>: activation of the <b>hypothalamic–pituitary–gonadal (HPG) axis</b>, ending in <b>reproductive competence</b>. <b>Adrenarche</b>: activation of the hypothalamic–pituitary–<b>adrenal</b> axis, often <b>earlier</b>. Growth spurt ~12 in girls, ~14 in boys.","w":["HPA.","That is adrenarche.","Not reproductive.","Adrenarche is earlier."],"k":"Gonadarche (HPG) vs adrenarche (HPA).","rn":[["","Gonadarche","Gonadarche","Begins with activation of the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis; Ends with attainment of reproductive competence"]]},{"id":12048,"t":"Puberty and Pubertal Timing","s":"Why are girls in late primary school often taller than boys?","o":["Girls’ growth spurt is ~12, before sexual development; boys’ ~14","Boys’ growth spurt is ~12, before sexual development; girls’ ~14","Girls’ growth spurt is ~14, after sexual development; boys’ ~12","Boys stop growing earlier than girls","Girls have adrenarche before boys"],"e":"Growth axis activation: linear growth spurt at ~<b>12 in girls</b> (before secondary sexual development) and ~<b>14 in boys</b> (after genitals have begun to grow) – so girls are often taller in late primary school.","w":["Swapped.","Swapped.","Not stated.","Not the reason."],"k":"Growth spurt: girls ~12, boys ~14.","rn":[["","Why are girls often taller than boys in late primary school?","Why are girls often taller than boys in late primary school?","Earlier growth spurt"],["","Girls' growth spurt: before or after secondary sexual development?","Girls' growth spurt: before or after secondary sexual development?","Before (precedes it)"]]},{"id":12049,"t":"Puberty and Pubertal Timing","f":"in33","s":"In the HPG axis, which sequence of hormones is correct?","o":["Hypothalamus GnRH → pituitary LH/FSH → gonads, with negative feedback","Pituitary GnRH → hypothalamus LH/FSH → gonads, with negative feedback","Hypothalamus GnRH → pituitary LH/FSH → gonads, with positive feedback only","Hypothalamus LH/FSH → pituitary GnRH → gonads, with negative feedback","Hypothalamus GnRH → adrenal LH/FSH → gonads, with negative feedback"],"e":"HPG axis: <b>hypothalamus (GnRH)</b> → <b>anterior pituitary (LH, FSH)</b> → <b>testes (testosterone)</b> or <b>ovaries (oestrogen, progesterone)</b>, with <b>negative feedback</b> to the hypothalamus and pituitary.","w":["GnRH from hypothalamus.","Negative.","Swapped.","Pituitary."],"k":"HPG axis sequence.","rn":[["","What do the gonadal hormones do to the hypothalamus and pituitary?","What do the gonadal hormones do to the hypothalamus and pituitary?","Negative feedback"]]},{"id":12050,"t":"Puberty and Pubertal Timing","s":"A 7-year-old develops body odour and some body hair before true puberty. Name the process responsible.","e":"Adrenarche: HPA-driven release of adrenal androgens, often before gonadarche.","k":"Adrenarche.","a":["adrenarche"],"rn":[["","What 2 changes can adrenarche cause before typical puberty?","What 2 changes can adrenarche cause before typical puberty?","Body hair; Sweat and body odour changes"],["","What do the adrenal glands release in adrenarche?","What do the adrenal glands release in adrenarche?","Small amounts of androgens"],["","Adrenarche","Adrenarche","Activation of the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis; Often begins earlier than gonadarche"]]},{"id":12051,"t":"Puberty and Pubertal Timing","f":"in35","s":"How has the mean age of menarche changed?","o":["Norway: 17 (1840) → ~13 now; UK mean 12.9","Norway: 13 (1840) → ~17 now; UK mean 12.9","Norway: 17 (1840) → ~13 now; UK mean 10.9","Norway: 15 (1840) → ~11 now; UK mean 12.9","Norway: 17 (1840) → ~15 now; UK mean 14.9"],"e":"Menarche mean age: <b>Norway 1840 = 17, now 13</b> (Santrock, 1998); <b>UK now = 12.9</b> (Whincup et al., 2001). Age at puberty has <b>fallen</b> over the last few hundred years.","w":["Fallen.","12.9.","17.","13."],"k":"Menarche fell; UK 12.9.","rn":[["","What is mean age of menarche in the UK now?","What is mean age of menarche in the UK now?","12.9"]]},{"id":12052,"t":"Puberty and Pubertal Timing","s":"Which factors are given as causes of earlier puberty?","o":["Better living standards, health, nutrition and body mass","Better living standards, but less nutrition and body mass","Greater exam pressure and social media use","Less sleep and more screen time","Colder climates and higher altitude"],"e":"Causes: <b>improved standard of living</b>, <b>health and nutrition</b>, and <b>body mass</b> (girls ~<b>17% body fat</b> as a minimum threshold; boys > 2 SD).","w":["More nutrition.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Earlier puberty: living standards, nutrition, body fat.","rn":[["","What are the 3 causes of falling age at puberty?","What are the 3 causes of falling age at puberty?","Improved standard of living; Health and nutrition; Body mass"]]},{"id":12053,"t":"Puberty and Pubertal Timing","s":"Which statement about the range and sex differences in pubertal timing is correct?","o":["A 7-year range; 1–2 years earlier in girls","A 7-year range; 1–2 years earlier in boys","A 2-year range; 1–2 years earlier in girls","A 7-year range; 5 years earlier in girls","A 1-year range; identical in both sexes"],"e":"There is a <b>7-year range</b> for the onset of puberty, and it begins <b>1–2 years earlier for girls</b> than boys.","w":["Girls.","7-year.","1–2.","Not."],"k":"7-year range; girls 1–2 y earlier.","rn":[["","How much earlier does puberty begin in girls than boys?","How much earlier does puberty begin in girls than boys?","1-2 years"],["","What is the range for onset of puberty?","What is the range for onset of puberty?","7 years"]]},{"id":12054,"t":"Puberty and Pubertal Timing","s":"A girl reaches menarche at 10. Which health implications are linked to an earlier age of menarche?","o":["Teenage pregnancy and longer exposure to menstrual hormones","Teenage pregnancy and shorter exposure to menstrual hormones","Shorter adult height and later menopause","Lower risk of reproductive cancers","No implications for health"],"e":"Earlier menarche has implications for <b>teenage pregnancy</b> and <b>longer lifetime exposure to menstrual hormones</b> (possible links to reproductive cancers).","w":["Longer.","Not stated.","Possible higher risk.","There are."],"k":"Earlier menarche implications.","rn":[["","What 2 implications does earlier menarche have?","What 2 implications does earlier menarche have?","Unintended teenage pregnancy; Longer exposure to menstrual hormones"]]},{"id":12055,"t":"Physical Development During Puberty","s":"Which physical change during puberty is correctly described?","o":["Weight gain is mainly muscle in boys, fat in girls","Weight gain is mainly fat in boys, muscle in girls","Height increases by about 1 cm per year","Sleep need falls to about 5 hours","Voice changes occur only in girls"],"e":"Puberty: gonads secrete testosterone and oestradiol at ~11–14; rapid height/weight increase (up to 10 cm/year); weight gain mainly <b>muscle for boys</b>, <b>fat for girls</b>; voice changes for boys; sleep often longer (~9½ h).","w":["Swapped.","Up to 10 cm.","Not stated.","Boys."],"k":"Pubertal body composition changes.","rn":[["","Weight gain in puberty: boys vs girls?","Weight gain in puberty: boys vs girls?","Boys: mainly muscle; Girls: mainly fat"]]},{"id":12056,"t":"Adolescent Brain Development","f":"in37","s":"What happens to grey and white matter during adolescence (Gogtay et al. 2004)?","o":["Grey matter decreases; white matter increases","Grey matter increases; white matter decreases","Grey and white matter both increase","Grey and white matter both decrease","Grey matter decreases; white matter stays the same"],"e":"<b>Grey matter decreases</b> over time and <b>white matter increases</b>. <b>Synaptic pruning</b> strengthens frequently used connections and eliminates infrequently used ones, making connections more specialised and efficient.","w":["Reversed.","Grey falls.","White rises.","White rises."],"k":"Adolescence: grey ↓, white ↑.","rn":[["","What happens to grey and white matter over time?","What happens to grey and white matter over time?","Grey matter decreases; White matter increases"]]},{"id":12057,"t":"Adolescent Brain Development","s":"Which brain areas mature first and which last?","o":["Movement and senses first; memory and attention last","Memory and attention first; movement and senses last","All areas mature at the same time","Language first; vision and movement last","Vision first; movement and senses last"],"e":"Areas for <b>movement, vision and senses mature first</b>; areas for <b>memory and attention mature later</b>. The Gogtay figure shows pruning reaching the <b>front (prefrontal cortex) last</b>.","w":["Reversed.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Movement first."],"k":"Back-to-front maturation.","rn":[["","Which parts of the brain mature first?","Which parts of the brain mature first?","Physical movement; Vision; Senses"],["","Which parts of the brain mature later?","Which parts of the brain mature later?","Memory; Attention"]]},{"id":12058,"t":"Adolescent Brain Development","s":"Which functions are performed by the prefrontal cortex, which continues developing into the 20s?","o":["Executive functions: planning and control","Primary sensory functions: vision and hearing","Automatic functions: breathing and heart rate","Motor reflexes and balance","Language comprehension only"],"e":"The <b>prefrontal cortex</b> continues developing <b>into the 20s</b> (~25) and is responsible for <b>executive functions</b>: planning, problem solving, weighing consequences, assuming responsibility, interpreting and controlling emotions, <b>inhibitory control</b>.","w":["Not prefrontal.","Brainstem.","Not prefrontal.","Not stated."],"k":"Prefrontal = executive function; matures ~25.","rn":[["","What are the 6 executive functions of the prefrontal cortex?","What are the 6 executive functions of the prefrontal cortex?","Planning, organising thoughts; Problem solving; Weighing consequences; Assuming responsibility; Interpreting & controlling emotions; Inhibitory control"]]},{"id":12059,"t":"Adolescent Brain Development","s":"Why might some neurodiversity and learning difficulties be harder to identify in early childhood?","o":["Memory and attention mature later","Vision and movement mature later","Grey matter is absent in childhood","White matter decreases in childhood","Theory of mind emerges at birth"],"e":"Since <b>memory and attention</b> mature later, some neurodiversity and learning difficulties are harder to identify in early childhood.","w":["First.","Not stated.","Increases.","Not stated."],"k":"Late-maturing executive functions.","rn":[["","Why may neurodiversity be harder to diagnose in early childhood?","Why may neurodiversity be harder to diagnose in early childhood?","Memory and attention (executive function) mature later"],["","Which parts of the brain mature later?","Which parts of the brain mature later?","Memory; Attention"]]},{"id":12060,"t":"Adolescent Brain Development","s":"What is synaptic pruning?","o":["Removing little-used connections, strengthening used ones","Removing frequently used connections to save energy for new growth","Growing new neurons in the prefrontal cortex","Myelinating all connections equally","Increasing grey matter through new synapses"],"e":"<b>Synaptic pruning</b>: frequently used connections are <b>strengthened</b>, infrequently used ones are <b>eliminated</b>, reducing excess grey matter.","w":["Reversed.","Not stated.","Not equal.","Reduces grey."],"k":"Synaptic pruning definition.","rn":[["","Synaptic Pruning","Synaptic Pruning","Pruning of excess grey matter; Frequently used connections strengthened; Infrequently used connections eliminated"]]},{"id":12061,"t":"Health Risks in Adolescence","s":"Which statement about eating disorders is correct?","o":["Anorexia: 1.5% of females, 0.1% of males; onset 15–25","Anorexia: 15% of females, 1% of males; onset 15–25","Anorexia: 1.5% of females, 0.1% of males; onset 25–40","Bulimia: 1.5% of females, 0.1% of males; onset 15–25","Anorexia: 1.9% of females, 0.3% of males; onset 15–25"],"e":"<b>Anorexia</b>: 1.5% of females, 0.1% of males; mean onset <b>15–25</b>. <b>Bulimia</b>: 1.9% of females, 0.3% of males; more often identified in males and at an older age.","w":["1.5%.","15–25.","Bulimia 1.9%/0.3%.","Those are bulimia figures."],"k":"Eating disorder figures.","rn":[["","What is the mean age of onset of anorexia?","What is the mean age of onset of anorexia?","15-25 years"]]},{"id":12062,"t":"Health Risks in Adolescence","s":"What is the typical onset of schizophrenia?","o":["Late teens–early 20s","Early childhood","Mid-30s to mid-40s","After 60","Infancy"],"e":"<b>Schizophrenia</b>: typical onset <b>late teens to early 20s</b>; not fully understood (likely a biology–environment interplay).","w":["Not.","Not stated.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Schizophrenia onset: late teens–early 20s.","rn":[["","What is the typical onset of schizophrenia?","What is the typical onset of schizophrenia?","Late teens to early 20s"]]},{"id":12063,"t":"Health Risks in Adolescence","s":"How did overweight in 12–19-year-olds change between 1971 and 2002?","o":["Boys 6.1% → 16.7%; girls 6.2% → 15.4%","Boys 6.1% → 15.4%; girls 6.2% → 16.7%","Boys 16.7% → 6.1%; girls 15.4% → 6.2%","Boys 6.1% → 26.7%; girls 6.2% → 25.4%","Boys 6.1% → 16.7%; girls unchanged at 6.2%"],"e":"Overweight (12–19, 1971–2002): <b>boys 6.1% → 16.7%</b>; <b>girls 6.2% → 15.4%</b>. Physical activity <b>drops dramatically</b> in adolescence.","w":["Swapped.","Rose.","Not.","Rose."],"k":"Adolescent overweight rose.","rn":[["","How did overweight in boys change (1971-2002)?","How did overweight in boys change (1971-2002)?","6.1% to 16.7%"]]},{"id":12064,"t":"Health Risks in Adolescence","s":"Why might physical activity fall during adolescence, beyond biology?","o":["Body discomfort, school sport, low confidence, exams","Body discomfort, more energy and more compulsory sport","Falling muscle mass and falling sleep needs","More free time and fewer exam demands","Biology alone explains the fall"],"e":"Falling activity isn't only biological: <b>discomfort with a changing body</b>, <b>public school sports</b>, <b>low confidence</b>, and <b>exam and school demands</b> all play a part.","w":["Not stated.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Not only biology."],"k":"Social reasons for falling activity.","rn":[["","What non-biological factors reduce activity in adolescence?","What non-biological factors reduce activity in adolescence?","Uncomfortable with developing body; Sports in public forum at school; Lack of confidence; Exam and school demands"]]},{"id":12065,"t":"Cognitive, Social and Moral Development","s":"A teenager refuses to go to school with a small spot on her face, convinced everyone will stare. Name Elkind's concept.","e":"Imaginary audience (Elkind): belief that everyone is watching and judging; social media makes it harder to escape.","k":"Imaginary audience.","a":["imaginary audience","the imaginary audience"],"rn":[["","Imaginary Audience","Imaginary Audience","Feeling everyone is watching; Adolescents relax when removed from it"],["","What makes escaping the imaginary audience harder today?","What makes escaping the imaginary audience harder today?","Social media and technology"]]},{"id":12066,"t":"Cognitive, Social and Moral Development","s":"A teenager insists “nobody has ever felt as heartbroken as I do.” Name Elkind's concept.","e":"Personal fable (Elkind): belief that one's experiences and feelings are unique.","k":"Personal fable.","a":["personal fable","the personal fable"],"rn":[["","\"No one has ever felt like this before\" is an example of what?","\"No one has ever felt like this before\" is an example of what?","Personal fable"],["","Personal Fable","Personal Fable","Believe own beliefs and feelings are unique; Never before experienced by anyone"]]},{"id":12067,"t":"Cognitive, Social and Moral Development","s":"Which consequences of adolescent egocentrism are described?","o":["Conforming to avoid embarrassment, lower self-esteem, need for privacy","Conforming to avoid embarrassment, higher self-esteem, need for company","Higher confidence and resilience, less need for privacy","Little interest in what others think of them","Improved conservation and reversibility"],"e":"Adolescent egocentrism: going to extreme lengths to avoid embarrassment (conforming in clothes/behaviour), a <b>measurable drop in self-esteem</b> (hyper-aware), and a <b>need for privacy</b>. Young children tend to be resilient and confident; this switches in adolescence.","w":["Self-esteem drops.","Not.","Preoccupied.","Piaget."],"k":"Effects of adolescent egocentrism.","rn":[["","What are the 5 features of adolescent egocentrism?","What are the 5 features of adolescent egocentrism?","Preoccupied with what others think; Avoid embarrassment (conforming); Drop in self-esteem; Need for privacy; Personal fable"]]},{"id":12068,"t":"Cognitive, Social and Moral Development","s":"Why might social media intensify adolescent difficulties?","o":["It makes the imaginary audience harder to escape","It removes the imaginary audience altogether","It speeds up synaptic pruning","It reduces peer influence on teenagers","It brings puberty forward"],"e":"<b>Social media</b> makes it much harder to <b>escape the imaginary audience</b>; adolescents feel constantly scrutinised.","w":["Harder.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Social media and imaginary audience.","rn":[["","What makes escaping the imaginary audience harder today?","What makes escaping the imaginary audience harder today?","Social media and technology"]]},{"id":12069,"t":"Cognitive, Social and Moral Development","s":"What is Erikson's key adolescent stage, and what is one limitation?","o":["Identity vs role confusion; over-optimistic age norms","Identity vs role confusion; it ignored adolescents altogether","Trust vs mistrust; age norms were over-optimistic","Industry vs inferiority; it ignored culture","Identity vs role confusion; it was too cultural"],"e":"<b>Erikson</b>: <b>identity vs role confusion</b> – forging a coherent sense of self (“Who am I?”); role confusion when aspects can't be put together. Limitation: <b>age norms were overly optimistic</b>.","w":["About adolescents.","Infancy.","Not stated.","Limitation was age norms."],"k":"Erikson: identity vs role confusion.","rn":[["","What is the limitation of Erikson's theory?","What is the limitation of Erikson's theory?","Age norms overly optimistic; Complete identity by 20 years"],["","What is the adolescent stage in Erikson's theory?","What is the adolescent stage in Erikson's theory?","Identity versus role-confusion"]]},{"id":12070,"t":"Cognitive, Social and Moral Development","s":"A teenager keeps switching clothing styles, music tastes and friendship groups. Name Erikson's adolescent stage.","e":"Erikson's adolescent stage is identity vs role confusion; a limitation is over-optimistic age norms.","k":"Identity vs role confusion.","a":["identity vs role confusion","identity versus role confusion","identity v role confusion","identity role confusion","identity vs role diffusion","identity"],"rn":[["","What is the adolescent stage in Erikson's theory?","What is the adolescent stage in Erikson's theory?","Identity versus role-confusion"],["","What does identity vs role-confusion describe?","What does identity vs role-confusion describe?","Adolescent's pursuit of a coherent sense of self"],["","What question sums up identity vs role-confusion?","What question sums up identity vs role-confusion?","\"Who am I?\"; Forging an identity"]]},{"id":12071,"t":"Cognitive, Social and Moral Development","s":"Which statement about Kohlberg's theory of moral development is correct?","o":["Six stages in an invariant sequence across three levels","Four stages in an invariant sequence across two levels","Six stages that can occur in any order","Six stages that are culturally variable and fluid","Three stages across six levels"],"e":"<b>Kohlberg</b> builds on <b>Piaget</b>: <b>six stages</b> in an <b>invariant sequence</b> (each evolves from and replaces the one before), in <b>three levels</b>: <b>preconventional</b>, <b>conventional</b>, <b>postconventional (principled)</b>.","w":["Six, three.","Invariant.","Invariant.","Six stages, three levels."],"k":"Kohlberg: 6 stages, 3 levels.","rn":[["","What type of sequence is Kohlberg's theory?","What type of sequence is Kohlberg's theory?","Invariant sequence"],["","How many stages are in Kohlberg's theory?","How many stages are in Kohlberg's theory?","Six"],["","Why is Kohlberg's sequence invariant?","Why is Kohlberg's sequence invariant?","Each stage depends on certain cognitive abilities"]]},{"id":12072,"t":"Cognitive, Social and Moral Development","s":"A person disobeys an unjust law because it conflicts with their own ethical principles. Name Kohlberg's level of moral reasoning.","e":"Kohlberg: six stages in an invariant sequence across three levels: preconventional (punishment/reward), conventional (social rules, approval), postconventional (own ethical principles).","k":"Postconventional.","a":["postconventional","post-conventional","post conventional"],"rn":[["","What are Kohlberg's 3 levels of moral development?","What are Kohlberg's 3 levels of moral development?","Level 1: Preconventional; Level 2: Conventional; Level 3: Postconventional (Principled)"]]},{"id":12073,"t":"Piaget: Stage Theory","s":"Which reasoning best fits conventional morality?","o":["Considering others’ views and social rules for approval","Considering own ethical principles above social rules","Avoiding punishment and gaining personal rewards","Wanting wrongdoers punished harshly","Following no consistent moral rules"],"e":"<b>Conventional</b> morality takes <b>others' opinions and beliefs</b> into account – following social rules and approval. Preconventional is more likely to want others punished.","w":["Postconventional.","Preconventional.","More preconventional.","Not stated."],"k":"Conventional = social approval.","rn":[["","What are the 5 points of Vygotsky's Sociocultural Theory?","What are the 5 points of Vygotsky's Sociocultural Theory?","Learning is a social process; Mentors and peers influence learning; Cultural beliefs affect learning; Learning from others integrated individually; Culture provides 'tools of intellectual adaptation'"],["","What social behaviour appears as children recognise others may not understand things?","What social behaviour appears as children recognise others may not understand things?","Lying (more efficient lying)"]]},{"id":12074,"t":"Adolescence: Potential, Peers and Risk","s":"A 15-year-old picks up new skills very quickly but also takes dangerous risks. Which pairing describes adolescence?","o":["Heightened neuroplasticity alongside heightened risk taking","Reduced neuroplasticity alongside reduced risk taking","Heightened neuroplasticity alongside reduced risk taking","Reduced neuroplasticity alongside heightened risk taking","No change in cognition, but more risk taking"],"e":"Enormous potential: <b>heightened neuroplasticity</b> (sensitive period for second languages, instruments), enhanced <b>multitasking</b>, <b>critical thinking</b>. But also “<b>chaos</b>”, <b>heightened risk taking</b>, and struggling to focus on all changes.","w":["Heightened.","More risk.","Heightened.","Potential."],"k":"Adolescence: potential and chaos.","rn":[["","What are the downsides of adolescence?","What are the downsides of adolescence?","Period of \"chaos\" and great conflict; Heightened risk taking; Struggle to focus; abandon/ignore some skills"],["","What are the 3 areas of enormous potential in adolescence?","What are the 3 areas of enormous potential in adolescence?","Heightened neuroplasticity; Multitask and parallel processing; Critical thinking, self-aware, self-critical"]]},{"id":12075,"t":"Adolescence: Potential, Peers and Risk","s":"Which statement about peers in adolescence is correct?","o":["By 13, 90% report a same-sex best friend","By 13, 50% report a same-sex best friend","By 13, adolescents spend more time with family","By 13, peers have little influence","By 13, gender identity weakens"],"e":"Peers: friendships more important; gender identity intensifies; more time with <b>friends than family</b>; by <b>13, 90%</b> report a same-sex best friend; romantic relationships matter; friends influence <b>positively and negatively</b>, possibly more than parents.","w":["90%.","Friends.","Influential.","Intensifies."],"k":"Peer influence in adolescence.","rn":[["","What are the 4 points on the impact of peers?","What are the 4 points on the impact of peers?","Gender identity intensifies; More time with friends than family; 90% same-sex best friend by 13; Romantic relationships important"]]},{"id":12076,"t":"Adolescence: Potential, Peers and Risk","s":"Adolescents have improved decision-making skills, yet appear “good at making bad decisions”. Which evidence supports this?","o":["Accidents lead deaths, plus sexual and health risks","Infections are the leading cause of death in adolescents","Adolescents rarely take risks with their health","Accidents are rare, but sexual risk-taking is common","Adolescents cannot identify alternatives or likely consequences"],"e":"Adolescents can identify alternatives, consequences, desirability and likelihood. But <b>accidents are the leading cause of death</b> (Heron, 2007), <b>sexual risk-taking</b> (James et al., 2012) and <b>unhealthy behaviours</b> (de Visser et al., 2006). “Young male syndrome” describes risk-driven deaths in males.","w":["Accidents.","They do.","Leading cause.","They can."],"k":"Good skills, bad decisions.","rn":[["","What are the 3 examples of adolescent bad decisions?","What are the 3 examples of adolescent bad decisions?","Accidents; Sexual risk-taking; Unhealthy behaviours"]]},{"id":12077,"t":"Adolescence: Potential, Peers and Risk","s":"According to Patalay and Gage (2019), which adolescent behaviours have declined and which increased?","o":["Fewer: substance use, antisocial acts, pregnancy; more: MH problems","Fewer: mental health problems; more: substance use and teen pregnancy","Fewer: all risks, including mental health problems","More: all risks, including teen pregnancy","Fewer: self-harm; more: smoking and alcohol"],"e":"<b>Declined</b>: substance use (smoking, alcohol), antisocial behaviour (graffiti), sexual activity and teen pregnancies. <b>Increased</b>: <b>mental health problems</b>, <b>self-harm and suicide</b>, conduct disorders and attention issues. Reported mental health issues <b>doubled</b> over 10 years.","w":["Swapped.","MH rose.","Substance fell.","Swapped."],"k":"Patalay & Gage risk changes.","rn":[["","What 3 risk behaviours have significantly declined?","What 3 risk behaviours have significantly declined?","Substance use (smoking and alcohol); Antisocial behaviour (graffiti); Sexual activity and teenage pregnancies"]]},{"id":12078,"t":"Adolescence: Potential, Peers and Risk","s":"Which possible risk factors were suggested for the rise in adolescent mental health problems (Patalay, 2019)?","o":["Austerity, academic pressure, the digital age and COVID","More youth clubs, less screen time and COVID","Austerity, lower academic expectations and less screen time","Earlier bedtimes and fewer exams","Genetic changes in the population"],"e":"Possible risk factors: <b>austerity</b> (cuts to youth clubs), <b>academic pressure</b> (GCSE changes), the <b>digital-age environment</b> (can't escape the imaginary audience), the <b>COVID pandemic</b>. Adolescents spend <b>over 7 hours a day</b> on entertainment media.","w":["Cuts.","More pressure.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Mental health risk factors.","rn":[["","What are the 4 risk factors correlating with the findings?","What are the 4 risk factors correlating with the findings?","Austerity; Academic pressure; Digital age \"environment\"; COVID pandemic; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":12079,"t":"Adolescence: Potential, Peers and Risk","s":"How much time do children and adolescents spend on entertainment media, according to Strasburger et al. (2014)?","o":["Over 7 hours a day","About 1 hour a day","About 30 minutes a day","Over 15 hours a day","None"],"e":"Children and adolescents spend <b>over 7 hours a day</b> on entertainment media.","w":["Too low.","Too low.","Too high.","Not stated."],"k":">7 h/day media.","rn":[["","How long do children and adolescents spend on entertainment media a day?","How long do children and adolescents spend on entertainment media a day?","More than 7 hours; Also given as more than 8 hours"]]},{"id":12080,"t":"Medical Consultations with Adolescents","s":"According to Rutishauser et al. (2003), what proportion of adolescents wanted to see the doctor alone, and how many were given the chance?","o":["Up to 52% wanted to; only 20% had the chance","Up to 20% wanted to; 52% had the chance","Up to 90% wanted to; only 20% had the chance","Up to 52% wanted to; 52% had the chance","Up to 52% wanted to; only 5% had the chance"],"e":"Up to <b>52%</b> (up to age 15) said seeing the doctor <b>alone</b> was important, but only <b>20%</b> were given the chance. Adolescents can be dissatisfied due to <b>privacy, confidentiality</b> and <b>embarrassment</b>.","w":["Swapped.","52%.","20%.","20%."],"k":"52% want to be seen alone; 20% are.","rn":[["","What are the 3 reasons adolescents are dissatisfied?","What are the 3 reasons adolescents are dissatisfied?","Privacy and confidentiality concerns; Embarrassment about sensitive issues; Not given chance to see doctor alone"]]},{"id":12081,"t":"Medical Consultations with Adolescents","s":"A 15-year-old attends with a parent about a sensitive issue. Which approach is most appropriate?","o":["Offer time alone and explain confidentiality and its limits","Offer time alone and promise complete confidentiality","Speak mainly to the parent about the sensitive issue","Keep the parent present throughout and avoid raising the sensitive topic","Discuss it with the parent first, then the teenager"],"e":"Practical points: offer to see the adolescent <b>alone</b> for part of the consultation, explain <b>confidentiality</b> (and its limits), and be aware of the <b>imaginary audience</b> and embarrassment.","w":["Limits.","Talk to the teenager.","Avoiding is wrong.","Not stated."],"k":"See adolescents alone; explain confidentiality.","rn":[["","What are the 3 reasons adolescents are dissatisfied?","What are the 3 reasons adolescents are dissatisfied?","Privacy and confidentiality concerns; Embarrassment about sensitive issues; Not given chance to see doctor alone"]]},{"id":12082,"t":"Medical Consultations with Adolescents","s":"At what age can adolescents be seen privately about their own medical decisions, and what applies before that?","o":["At 16; before that they may be Gillick competent","At 18; before that they may be Gillick competent","At 16; before that they cannot be seen privately","At 12; no assessment is needed","At 14; before that parents must attend"],"e":"At <b>16</b> adolescents can be seen privately about their own medical decisions; before that they may be <b>Gillick competent</b>. Legal and ethical aspects are covered in Year 2.","w":["16.","May be competent.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"16 = private decisions; Gillick before.","rn":[["","Which concept is linked to age 16 for private consultations?","Which concept is linked to age 16 for private consultations?","Gillick competence"]]},{"id":12083,"t":"Theory of Mind","s":"A 5-year-old having a blood test is told by the doctor: “I can't feel what you feel, so tell me where it hurts.” Which concept is the doctor supporting?","o":["Theory of mind","Conservation","Object permanence","Formal operational reasoning","Egocentric speech"],"e":"Young children may assume the doctor already knows; theory of mind is <b>active</b>, so helping them (“Dr can't know your tummy hurts”) develops communication from ~5–7.","w":["Not stated.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Support ToM in consultations.","rn":[["","What are the 3 implications of poor theory of mind for consultations?","What are the 3 implications of poor theory of mind for consultations?","Describing their own feelings to someone who doesn't know; Understanding roles and perspectives of people present; Theory of mind is an active process"],["","What does extended theory of mind look like?","What does extended theory of mind look like?","\"I know that you know that I know\"; e.g. gossip"],["","Children younger than what age may not have a well-developed theory of mind?","Children younger than what age may not have a well-developed theory of mind?","7"]]},{"id":12084,"t":"Children's Understanding of Illness","s":"A play therapist uses a teddy bear to show a child how an anaesthetic will be given. Which principles does this apply?","o":["Creative, age-appropriate communication","Formal, abstract explanation for all ages","Detailed technical language to reassure parents","Distraction so the child does not ask questions","Testing the child’s conservation skills"],"e":"Use <b>creative, age-appropriate</b> communication matched to <b>stage and experience</b>; play therapists use teddies (e.g. Teddy Bear Hospital) to demonstrate procedures.","w":["Not abstract.","Not stated.","Encourage questions.","Not stated."],"k":"Play as communication.","rn":[["","What are the 5 points of Step 2?","What are the 5 points of Step 2?","Assess developmental stage; Match explanation to age and experience; Creative, age-appropriate communication; Consider language and concepts used; Attention to the child in the moment"]]},{"id":12085,"t":"Vygotsky: Sociocultural Theory","s":"Which pair of theorists would most disagree about the role of culture in development?","o":["Piaget and Vygotsky","Bibace and Walsh","Elkind and Erikson","Kohlberg and Piaget","Gogtay and Patalay"],"e":"<b>Piaget</b>: little role for culture (universal stages). <b>Vygotsky</b>: development differs between cultures (sociocultural).","w":["Co-authors.","Not culture.","Kohlberg built on Piaget.","Not theorists of culture."],"k":"Piaget vs Vygotsky on culture.","rn":[["","Piaget vs Vygotsky: development across cultures?","Piaget vs Vygotsky: development across cultures?","Piaget: stages discrete and universal; Vygotsky: differs between cultures"],["","Vygotsky's stages vs Piaget's stages: how fixed?","Vygotsky's stages vs Piaget's stages: how fixed?","Vygotsky: fluid, less fixed; Piaget: discrete, universal"]]},{"id":12086,"t":"Piaget: Stage Theory","s":"A 12-year-old can discuss how stopping exercise now might affect health later. Which stage does this future planning reflect?","o":["Formal operational","Concrete operational","Pre-operational","Sensorimotor","Phenomenism"],"e":"Formal operational thinkers consider <b>hypothetical</b> outcomes and future planning (“if I do this now, it may help me later”).","w":["Not abstract.","Not logical.","Pre-verbal.","Illness concept."],"k":"Future planning = formal operational.","rn":[["","What future thinking appears in the formal operational stage?","What future thinking appears in the formal operational stage?","Future planning; Doing something now may help later"]]},{"id":12087,"t":"Children's Understanding of Illness","s":"A 9-year-old needs an explanation of why they must take an inhaler. Which approach best fits their likely understanding?","o":["Concrete explanations, not stress-based ones","A detailed explanation of psychophysiology","Abstract hypothetical scenarios about the lungs","No explanation, only instructions to parents","An explanation linking illness to bad behaviour"],"e":"A 9-year-old is likely <b>concrete operational</b> and around the <b>contamination/internalisation</b> stage: explain concretely; avoid abstract and psychophysiological ideas; never reinforce illness-as-punishment beliefs.","w":["16+.","Concrete.","Explain to the child.","Never."],"k":"Match explanation to stage.","rn":[["","Which psychological and lifestyle factors feature in psychophysiological explanations o…","Which psychological and lifestyle factors feature in psychophysiological explanations of illness?","Stress; Fatigue; Alcohol use; Drug use"]]},{"id":12088,"t":"Vygotsky: Sociocultural Theory","s":"Which statement about the universality of development stages is the modern view?","o":["Broadly universal, but neglected children fall behind","Entirely culturally determined, with no universal order of stages","Universal and fixed, unaffected by neglect","Not real; development is continuous","Biologically fixed; social factors are minor"],"e":"Today stages are recognised as <b>broadly universal</b>, but parents, peers and culture are very important: <b>isolated, abused or not spoken to</b> children fall behind.","w":["Broadly universal.","Neglect matters.","Stages recognised.","Social matters."],"k":"Universal stages + social influence.","rn":[["","What is recognised today about the two theories?","What is recognised today about the two theories?","Stages are universal; But parents, peers and culture are very important; Both matter"]]},{"id":12089,"t":"Bowlby: Attachment Theory","s":"Which pairing of theorist and concept is correct?","o":["Bowlby – secure base","Vygotsky – object permanence","Piaget – zone of proximal development","Elkind – identity vs role confusion","Erikson – personal fable"],"e":"Bowlby/Howe: <b>secure base</b>. Piaget: object permanence. Vygotsky: ZPD. Erikson: identity vs role confusion. Elkind: imaginary audience and personal fable.","w":["Piaget.","Vygotsky.","Erikson.","Elkind."],"k":"Match theorists to concepts.","rn":[["","What does a secure attachment provide?","What does a secure attachment provide?","A 'Secure Base'"],["","What does the child do from the 'Secure Base'?","What does the child do from the 'Secure Base'?","Begins to explore the world"]]},{"id":12090,"t":"Puberty and Pubertal Timing","s":"A girl reaches menarche at 11. Which factors might have contributed?","o":["Good nutrition and ~17% body fat","Poor nutrition and low body mass","Good nutrition, but body fat below 5%","Lack of growth hormone","Living in 1840 Norway"],"e":"Earlier puberty is linked to an <b>improved standard of living</b>, <b>health and nutrition</b>, and <b>body mass</b> (girls ~17% body fat).","w":["Delays.","~17%.","Not stated.","17 then."],"k":"Nutrition and body fat → earlier menarche.","rn":[["","What body fat do girls need for puberty?","What body fat do girls need for puberty?","~17% body fat"],["","What are the 3 causes of falling age at puberty?","What are the 3 causes of falling age at puberty?","Improved standard of living; Health and nutrition; Body mass"]]},{"id":12091,"t":"Puberty and Pubertal Timing","s":"Which statement about gonadarche and adrenarche timing is correct?","o":["Adrenarche often begins earlier than gonadarche","Gonadarche always begins earlier than adrenarche","They always begin together","Adrenarche occurs only in adults","Gonadarche involves the adrenal glands"],"e":"<b>Adrenarche</b> (HPA axis) often begins <b>earlier</b> than <b>gonadarche</b> (HPG axis).","w":["Adrenarche earlier.","Not always.","Childhood.","HPG."],"k":"Adrenarche before gonadarche.","rn":[["","Adrenarche","Adrenarche","Activation of the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis; Often begins earlier than gonadarche"]]},{"id":12092,"t":"Medical Consultations with Adolescents","s":"Why might an adolescent clinic patient be reluctant to discuss sexual health with a parent present?","o":["Privacy, confidentiality and embarrassment","They rarely have sexual health questions","They lack theory of mind in adolescence","They are in the sensorimotor stage","They want parents to answer for them"],"e":"Adolescents worry about <b>privacy and confidentiality</b>, <b>embarrassment</b>, and what will be <b>told to parents</b>; sexuality is a particularly sensitive area.","w":["They do.","They have it.","Not.","Not stated."],"k":"Privacy concerns in adolescents.","rn":[["","What are the 3 reasons adolescents are dissatisfied?","What are the 3 reasons adolescents are dissatisfied?","Privacy and confidentiality concerns; Embarrassment about sensitive issues; Not given chance to see doctor alone"]]},{"id":12093,"t":"Adolescent Brain Development","s":"Which statement about synaptic pruning and adolescent potential is consistent?","o":["Connections become specialised and efficient; heightened plasticity","Connections become fewer and less efficient; reduced plasticity","Frequently used connections are removed first; heightened plasticity results","Grey matter increases; heightened plasticity","Pruning happens only in infancy"],"e":"<b>Reorganisation</b>: connections become more specialised and efficient; adolescence is a period of <b>heightened neuroplasticity</b>.","w":["Efficient.","Little-used removed.","Grey decreases.","Adolescence too."],"k":"Pruning → efficiency.","rn":[["","Reorganisation (brain)","Reorganisation (brain)","Connections become more specialised and efficient"]]},{"id":12094,"t":"Vygotsky: Sociocultural Theory","s":"Which theory would best explain a child learning to use a spoon by working alongside an older sibling?","o":["Vygotsky: shared problem-solving within the ZPD","Piaget: solo exploration in the sensorimotor stage","Bowlby: secure base for exploration","Kohlberg: conventional morality","Elkind: the personal fable"],"e":"Vygotsky: children learn best through <b>problem-solving shared with someone else</b> (parent, peer), within the <b>zone of proximal development</b>.","w":["Social.","Not best fit.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Shared learning = Vygotsky.","rn":[["","What are the 6 key features of Vygotsky's theory?","What are the 6 key features of Vygotsky's theory?","Stages are fluid; Importance of play; Reasoning limited at any age; Full development needs social interaction; Shared problem-solving; Egocentric speech becomes inner speech"]]},{"id":12095,"t":"Theory of Mind","s":"A 4-year-old with tummy ache is asked where it hurts and replies, puzzled, “You know where.” What best explains this?","o":["Immature theory of mind: she assumes you share her feelings","Lack of object permanence: she cannot picture her abdomen","Contamination-stage thinking about the cause of illness","Lack of conservation of quantity and volume","Insecure attachment to the clinician"],"e":"Theory of mind (attributing beliefs and feelings to others) emerges around 3–5 years. Children under 7 may assume the doctor already knows how they feel, so ask directly and explain that you cannot feel what they feel.","w":["Object permanence develops in the sensorimotor stage (0–2).","That concerns the cause of illness, not who knows her feelings.","Conservation is about quantity, not other minds.","Attachment is not the issue here."],"k":"Young children assume you know what they feel.","rn":[["","Why may a young child not describe symptoms well?","Why may a young child not describe symptoms well?","Assumes the doctor already knows how they feel"],["","What does extended theory of mind look like?","What does extended theory of mind look like?","\"I know that you know that I know\"; e.g. gossip"],["","What can children learn as theory of mind develops?","What can children learn as theory of mind develops?","Communicate their feelings more accurately"]]},{"id":12096,"t":"Theory of Mind","s":"Which statement about how theory of mind extends into adult social life is correct?","o":["It extends to many levels, e.g. gossip","It stops developing at about age 5","It is mainly relevant to children with autism","It is irrelevant in adult social life","It is the same as conservation"],"e":"Theory of mind can extend to many levels (“I know that you know that I know...”), as in everyday <b>gossip</b>, a huge part of human social life.","w":["Continues.","All.","Huge part.","Different."],"k":"Multi-level ToM in social life.","rn":[["","What does extended theory of mind look like?","What does extended theory of mind look like?","\"I know that you know that I know\"; e.g. gossip"]]},{"id":12097,"t":"Piaget: Stage Theory","s":"Which Piagetian stage lacks symbolic thought?","o":["Sensorimotor","Pre-operational","Concrete operational","Formal operational","None"],"e":"<b>Sensorimotor</b> (0–2, pre-verbal): infants “think” by doing; <b>no symbolic thought</b>. Symbols emerge in the pre-operational stage.","w":["Symbols begin.","Logical thought.","Abstract.","One does."],"k":"Sensorimotor: no symbolic thought.","rn":[["","Sensorimotor stage: how do infants \"think\"?","Sensorimotor stage: how do infants \"think\"?","By doing"],["","Sensorimotor Stage","Sensorimotor Stage","Stage 1 of Piaget; Birth to ~2 years; Pre-verbal"],["","Sensorimotor stage: do infants think symbolically?","Sensorimotor stage: do infants think symbolically?","No"]]},{"id":12098,"t":"Piaget: Stage Theory","s":"Which statement about lying in the concrete operational stage is correct?","o":["Knowing others’ minds allows more effective lying","Lying is impossible because of egocentrism","Lying first appears in the sensorimotor stage of development","Lying reflects a lack of reversibility","Lying reflects egocentric speech"],"e":"In the concrete stage, children understand others have different mental worlds, which is also when <b>more effective lying</b> appears.","w":["Not stated.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Effective lying ↔ understanding minds.","rn":[["","What social behaviour appears as children recognise others may not understand things?","What social behaviour appears as children recognise others may not understand things?","Lying (more efficient lying)"]]},{"id":12099,"t":"Piaget: Stage Theory","s":"A 14-year-old can reason about “what if” situations and test hypotheses in her head. Name Piaget's stage.","e":"Formal operational (11+): abstract, hypothetical and deductive (general → specific) reasoning. Concrete operational children (7–11) reason logically only about real objects.","k":"Formal operational.","a":["formal operational","formal operations","formal operational stage","formal"],"rn":[["","Formal Operational Stage","Formal Operational Stage","Stage 4 of Piaget; ~11 upwards"],["","Formal operational stage: what develops?","Formal operational stage: what develops?","Abstract thinking"],["","What type of logic begins in the formal operational stage?","What type of logic begins in the formal operational stage?","Deductive logic; Reasoning from general principles"]]},{"id":12100,"t":"Children's Understanding of Illness","s":"What is the main principle when communicating with children of different ages?","o":["Adapt to developing abilities to aid understanding","Adapt to children’s abilities only after age 7","Treat children as small adults with simpler words","Use identical explanations for all ages","Focus on parents, as children’s understanding is fixed"],"e":"Key message: children have <b>developing intellectual abilities</b>, and practitioners should <b>adapt to these</b> to <b>increase comprehension</b> and <b>reduce the anxiety</b> of the child.","w":["All ages.","Not small adults.","Adapt.","Developing."],"k":"Adapt to children's abilities.","rn":[["","What 5 factors does children's understanding of illness depend on?","What 5 factors does children's understanding of illness depend on?","Age and stage of development; Language / communication style and ability; Cultural background; Personal experience; Systemic and family factors"]]},{"id":12101,"t":"Children's Understanding of Illness","s":"A 9-year-old with eczema says it came because he touched a dirty dog and had been naughty. Name the Bibace and Walsh stage.","e":"Contamination: illness caused by physical contact with a harmful person or object, or by bad behaviour. It follows contagion (illness from proximity) and precedes internalisation.","k":"Contamination.","a":["contamination"],"rn":[["","Contamination","Contamination","Age 7-9; Illness caused by physical contact with an ill person; May be seen as punishment for misbehaviour"],["","What are the 6 Bibace & Walsh stages of explaining illness (in order)?","What are the 6 Bibace & Walsh stages of explaining illness (in order)?","Phenomenism (2-4); Contagion (4-7); Contamination (7-9); Internalisation (9-11); Physiological (11-16); Psychophysiological (16+); MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":12102,"t":"Bowlby: Attachment Theory","s":"Which statement about children in incubator-free, isolated environments links Vygotsky and Bowlby?","o":["Lack of interaction and attachment can hold back cognition","Isolation from others improves independent cognitive development","Attachment is unrelated to cognitive development","Culture has no effect on isolated children","Peers are unimportant before adolescence"],"e":"Vygotsky: full development requires <b>social interaction</b>. Bowlby: <b>secure attachment</b> is vital to cognitive development. Isolated children fall behind.","w":["Not.","Vital.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Social interaction and attachment support cognition."},{"id":12103,"t":"Pain and Healthcare in Childhood","s":"Which statement about pain scales in older children is correct?","o":["Numerical or VAS scales; they help reluctant children","Face scales only; they help reluctant children","Numerical scales; older children cannot reliably self-report","Numerical scales; they are free of bias","No scales; ask the parents instead"],"e":"<b>Numerical or visual analogue scales</b> for older children/adolescents; scales also help older children who lack confidence or are unwilling to describe symptoms. Scales are subject to biases.","w":["Younger.","They can.","Biased.","Self-report."],"k":"Scales for older children.","rn":[["","What scales measure pain intensity in older children/adolescents?","What scales measure pain intensity in older children/adolescents?","Numerical scales; Visual analogue scales"]]},{"id":12104,"t":"Vygotsky: Sociocultural Theory","s":"A parent asks whether their child's thinking skills are “nature or nurture”. Which answer best reflects current thinking?","o":["Biology and social influence both shape development","Development reflects biology alone","Development reflects social influence alone, not biological maturation","Piaget proved social influence irrelevant","Vygotsky proved biology irrelevant"],"e":"The Piaget–Vygotsky contrast mirrors “nature vs nurture”, which the lecturer called <b>meaningless</b>: it is nature <b>by</b> nurture.","w":["Both.","Both.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Nature by nurture.","rn":[["","Piaget vs Vygotsky: what influences development?","Piaget vs Vygotsky: what influences development?","Piaget: interaction and exploration of the environment; Vygotsky: social factors"],["","What are the 5 points of Vygotsky's Sociocultural Theory?","What are the 5 points of Vygotsky's Sociocultural Theory?","Learning is a social process; Mentors and peers influence learning; Cultural beliefs affect learning; Learning from others integrated individually; Culture provides 'tools of intellectual adaptation'"],["","What social and environmental pressures affect adolescents?","What social and environmental pressures affect adolescents?","School pressure; Family disruption; Peer influence"]]},{"id":12105,"t":"Cognitive, Social and Moral Development","s":"When assessing what a child can tell you, which question is <b>not</b> relevant?","o":["Which Kohlberg level is the child at?","What can the child tell you about their symptoms?","How do they experience their symptoms?","What level of language can they use?","Does the child understand what is happening?"],"e":"Step 1: what can the child tell you about <b>symptoms</b>; how they <b>experience</b> them; their <b>level of language</b>; whether they <b>understand</b> what is happening.","w":["A step-1 question.","A step-1 question.","A step-1 question.","A step-1 question."],"k":"Step 1 questions.","rn":[["","What are Kohlberg's 3 levels of moral development?","What are Kohlberg's 3 levels of moral development?","Level 1: Preconventional; Level 2: Conventional; Level 3: Postconventional (Principled)"]]},{"id":12106,"t":"Children's Understanding of Illness","s":"Which action belongs to communicating information to a child?","o":["Match the explanation to age and experience","Ask the parent about symptoms","Take a full history from the child","Measure the child’s blood pressure","Assess the child’s development by tests"],"e":"Step 2: assess the developmental stage; <b>match explanations to age and experience</b>; use creative age-appropriate communication; consider language and concepts; attend to the child <b>in the moment</b>.","w":["Not this step.","Not this step.","Not stated.","Not step 2."],"k":"Step 2 strategies.","rn":[["","What are the 5 points of Step 2?","What are the 5 points of Step 2?","Assess developmental stage; Match explanation to age and experience; Creative, age-appropriate communication; Consider language and concepts used; Attention to the child in the moment"]]},{"id":12107,"t":"Physical Development During Puberty","s":"Which statement about girls' fat distribution at puberty is correct?","o":["Around buttocks and thighs, later supporting lactation","Around the abdomen, later supporting pregnancy","Around buttocks and thighs, with no later function","Fat is lost in girls during puberty","Fat distribution is the same in boys and girls"],"e":"Girls' fat is laid down around the <b>buttocks and thighs</b>, which can later <b>support lactation</b>.","w":["Buttocks/thighs.","Lactation.","Gained.","Different."],"k":"Pubertal fat and lactation.","rn":[["","Where is fat laid down in girls and why?","Where is fat laid down in girls and why?","Buttocks and thighs; Used for lactation after pregnancy"]]},{"id":12108,"t":"Puberty and Pubertal Timing","s":"Name the pubertal process in which activation of the HPG axis culminates in reproductive competence.","e":"Gonadarche: HPG axis activation (GnRH → LH/FSH → gonads) ending in reproductive competence. Adrenarche is HPA-driven adrenal androgen release and often begins earlier.","k":"Gonadarche.","a":["gonadarche"],"rn":[["","Adrenarche","Adrenarche","Activation of the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis; Often begins earlier than gonadarche"],["","Gonadarche","Gonadarche","Begins with activation of the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis; Ends with attainment of reproductive competence"],["","What are the 3 hormonal events of puberty?","What are the 3 hormonal events of puberty?","Gonadarche; Adrenarche; Activation of the growth axis"]]},{"id":12109,"t":"Adolescent Brain Development","s":"A 19-year-old acts impulsively and plans poorly. Name the brain region still maturing into the 20s that underlies planning and control.","e":"The prefrontal cortex (executive functions: planning and control) matures last, continuing into the 20s; movement and senses mature first.","k":"Prefrontal cortex.","a":["prefrontal cortex","pfc","prefrontal","frontal cortex"],"rn":[["","When does the prefrontal cortex develop?","When does the prefrontal cortex develop?","Early in life into the 20s"],["","What type of functions is the prefrontal cortex responsible for?","What type of functions is the prefrontal cortex responsible for?","Rational, executive brain functions"],["","At about what age is prefrontal cortex development complete?","At about what age is prefrontal cortex development complete?","About 25"]]},{"id":12110,"t":"Adolescent Brain Development","s":"MRI of a 14-year-old shows falling grey matter and rising white matter. Name the process that removes little-used synaptic connections.","e":"Synaptic pruning removes little-used connections and strengthens used ones, making networks specialised and efficient. Grey matter decreases and white matter increases in adolescence (Gogtay et al. 2004).","k":"Synaptic pruning.","a":["synaptic pruning","pruning","neural pruning"],"rn":[["","Image card: What does this show from age 5 to 20?","What does this show from age 5 to 20?","Grey matter volume decreasing; Synaptic pruning; Gogtay et al. (2004)"],["","Synaptic Pruning","Synaptic Pruning","Pruning of excess grey matter; Frequently used connections strengthened; Infrequently used connections eliminated"]]},{"id":12111,"t":"Vygotsky: Sociocultural Theory","s":"Which statement about technology's effect on adolescents is balanced?","o":["Not all bad, but constant observation matters","Entirely harmful for all adolescents, with no benefits","Entirely beneficial for adolescents","Has no real impact on adolescents","Only gaming has any effect"],"e":"Technology isn't all bad (some gaming can be positive), but cutting young people off from peers and <b>constant observation</b> has a big impact.","w":["Balanced.","Balanced.","Impact.","More."],"k":"Balanced view of technology.","rn":[["","What is recognised today about the two theories?","What is recognised today about the two theories?","Stages are universal; But parents, peers and culture are very important; Both matter"]]},{"id":12112,"t":"Vygotsky: Sociocultural Theory","s":"Which statement about Piaget is correct?","o":["Swiss (1896–1980); one of the first to study children’s intelligence","Russian (1896–1934); proposed the zone of proximal development","Swiss (1896–1980); proposed attachment theory and the idea of a secure base","British (1907–1990); proposed attachment theory","American; proposed the imaginary audience"],"e":"<b>Jean Piaget</b> (Swiss, 1896–1980): one of the first to really study children's intelligence. Vygotsky (Russian, 1896–1934) proposed the ZPD.","w":["Vygotsky.","Bowlby.","Bowlby.","Elkind."],"k":"Piaget: Swiss, 1896–1980."},{"id":12113,"t":"Piaget: Stage Theory","s":"What is the meaning of “operate” in “concrete operational”?","o":["To experiment with and manipulate real objects","To think abstractly about hypothetical ideas","To perform mental surgery on ideas","To imagine objects that are not present","To talk to oneself while working"],"e":"Concrete operational: learns to <b>experiment with and manipulate (operate) real (concrete) objects</b>.","w":["Formal.","Not.","Pre-operational.","Egocentric speech."],"k":"Operate = manipulate real objects.","rn":[["","What are the 7 features of the concrete operational stage?","What are the 7 features of the concrete operational stage?","Experiment with and manipulate real objects; Conservation of number, reversibility; Classification; organised thinking; Inductive logic; Still struggle with abstract/hypothetical; Less egocentric; Thoughts are unique"],["","What does \"concrete\" mean in concrete operational?","What does \"concrete\" mean in concrete operational?","Real objects"]]},{"id":12114,"t":"Pain and Healthcare in Childhood","s":"Which statement about anxiety in children's healthcare experiences is correct?","o":["Anxious adults tend to produce an anxious child","Anxious adults tend to calm a child down","Children are unaffected by adults’ emotions","Anxiety occurs only in adolescents","Anxiety improves cooperation with care"],"e":"Experiences are <b>mediated by the adults</b> around them: <b>anxious adults → anxious child</b>.","w":["Opposite.","Mediated.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Adult anxiety transmits to children.","rn":[["","What happens if the adults around a child are fearful or anxious?","What happens if the adults around a child are fearful or anxious?","The child can pick that up"]]},{"id":12115,"t":"Puberty and Pubertal Timing","s":"A 15-year-old gymnast with very low body fat has not started her periods. Below roughly what body-fat percentage may menarche be delayed?","o":["About 17%","About 7%","About 27%","About 37%","About 12%"],"e":"Girls ~<b>17% body fat</b> – a <b>minimum fat threshold</b> for female puberty to begin.","w":["17%.","17%.","17%.","17%."],"k":"~17% body fat threshold.","rn":[["","What body fat do girls need for puberty?","What body fat do girls need for puberty?","~17% body fat"]]},{"id":12116,"t":"Puberty and Pubertal Timing","s":"Name the hypothalamic hormone that starts the HPG axis cascade at puberty.","e":"Hypothalamus releases GnRH → pituitary releases LH and FSH → gonads produce sex hormones, with negative feedback.","k":"GnRH.","a":["gnrh","gonadotrophin releasing hormone","gonadotropin releasing hormone","gonadotrophin-releasing hormone","gonadotropin-releasing hormone"],"rn":[["","GnRH","GnRH","Gonadotrophin releasing hormone"],["","What hormone does the hypothalamus release in the HPG axis?","What hormone does the hypothalamus release in the HPG axis?","GnRH"]]},{"id":12117,"t":"Children's Understanding of Illness","s":"A 16-year-old with diabetes tells you that late nights and exam stress made their control worse. Which developmental understanding does this show?","o":["Psychophysiological","Physiological","Internalisation","Contamination","Formal operational"],"e":"<b>Psychophysiological (16+)</b>: understanding that stress and fatigue affect physiological processes.","w":["No stress link.","External.","Contact.","Piaget, not illness."],"k":"16+ psychophysiological.","rn":[["","Psychophysiological","Psychophysiological","Age 16+; Stress and fatigue can affect physiological processes; Rather than just being an outcome"]]},{"id":12118,"t":"Adolescence: Potential, Peers and Risk","s":"Which statement about “Young male syndrome” is correct?","o":["A bump in adolescent male deaths driven by risk taking","A bump in adolescent male deaths driven by infection","A genetic condition causing early male puberty","An eating disorder seen mainly in young males","A theory-of-mind deficit in young males"],"e":"<b>“Young male syndrome”</b>: a bump in death rates in adolescent males driven by <b>risk taking</b>.","w":["Risk.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Young male syndrome = risk-driven deaths.","rn":[["","Young Male Syndrome","Young Male Syndrome","Bump in death rates; In adolescent men"]]},{"id":12119,"t":"Medical Consultations with Adolescents","s":"Which combination best describes an adolescent consultation strategy that accounts for Elkind's ideas?","o":["Privacy, confidentiality and minimising embarrassment","Asking parents to answer sensitive questions on the teenager's behalf","Discussing sensitive issues in the waiting room","Ignoring embarrassment to save time","Asking peers for background information"],"e":"Because adolescents feel watched (<b>imaginary audience</b>) and need <b>privacy</b>, offer time alone, explain confidentiality and minimise embarrassment.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Privacy for adolescents.","rn":[["","What are the 3 reasons adolescents are dissatisfied?","What are the 3 reasons adolescents are dissatisfied?","Privacy and confidentiality concerns; Embarrassment about sensitive issues; Not given chance to see doctor alone"]]},{"id":12120,"t":"Medical Consultations with Adolescents","s":"Which statement best summarises adolescence?","o":["Big changes from neural, social and environmental factors","Major changes driven by hormones alone","Major physical changes but relatively few psychological ones","A period shared with all primates","A period that ends by about 12"],"e":"Summary: adolescence brings major <b>physiological and psychological</b> changes, driven by <b>neural changes</b> plus <b>social and environmental</b> factors, forming the young person's <b>identity</b>.","w":["More.","Both.","Sapiens only.","Not."],"k":"Adolescence summary.","rn":[["","What do adolescent cognitive changes and behaviours result from?","What do adolescent cognitive changes and behaviours result from?","Neural changes; Social and environmental factors"]]},{"id":12121,"t":"Piaget: Stage Theory","s":"A 5-year-old boy is waiting in clinic. A nurse pours his juice from a short wide cup into a tall thin cup in front of him. He insists the tall cup now has more juice. He also struggles to understand why the nurse cannot see the picture in his book from where she is sitting.<br><br>Which Piagetian stage is he in, and what does this mean for explaining a procedure to him?","o":["Pre-operational; use concrete, simple explanations from his point of view","Concrete operational; use logical, abstract explanations","Formal operational; discuss hypothetical outcomes with him","Sensorimotor; he cannot use language or symbols at all","Concrete operational, because he can classify objects; he will understand reversibility and others' views"],"e":"Step 1: lacking conservation (thinking the amount changes with the shape of the container) and egocentricity (struggling to see others' perspectives) are features of the pre-operational stage (2–7 years). Step 2: thinking at this stage is concrete and not logical, so explanations should be simple, concrete and age-appropriate rather than abstract or hypothetical.","w":["Concrete operational children have conservation and are less egocentric.","Abstract, hypothetical reasoning belongs to the formal operational stage (11+).","He is using language, which emerges in the pre-operational stage.","He lacks conservation, so he is not yet concrete operational."],"k":"Pre-operational: no conservation, egocentric → concrete explanations.","rn":[["","What are the 5 features of the pre-operational stage?","What are the 5 features of the pre-operational stage?","Emergence of language; Beginnings of symbolic thinking; Egocentricity; Concrete thinking; Lack of concept of conservation"],["","What are Piaget's 4 stages of intellectual development (in order)?","What are Piaget's 4 stages of intellectual development (in order)?","Sensorimotor; Pre-operational; Concrete operational; Formal operational; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":12122,"t":"Theory of Mind","s":"A 3-year-old girl watches a puppet show. Sally puts her ball in a basket and leaves; Anne moves it to a box. When asked where Sally will look, the girl points to the box. Later in a consultation, she becomes frustrated that the doctor \"doesn't know\" her tummy hurts, even though she has not told him.<br><br>What do both observations show, and what is the clinical implication?","o":["No theory of mind yet; she may not realise the doctor doesn't know how she feels","Full theory of mind; she understands Sally's false belief","Object permanence has not developed, so she forgets the ball","Conservation is missing, so she cannot locate the ball","She is in the formal operational stage, so she can reason about others' beliefs and will describe her own feelings fully"],"e":"Step 1: answering \"in the box\" in the Sally–Anne test shows no theory of mind yet: the child assumes Sally knows what they know; theory of mind (attributing beliefs and feelings to others) emerges around 3–5 years. Step 2: in consultations, children under 7 may struggle to describe their feelings to someone who does not know how they feel, so the doctor must actively ask.","w":["Pointing to the box shows she has not understood Sally's false belief.","Object permanence develops in the sensorimotor stage and is not what is tested.","The test is about others' beliefs, not conservation.","A 3-year-old is not in the formal operational stage."],"k":"Sally–Anne \"box\" = no theory of mind; children may assume the doctor knows.","rn":[["","What are the 3 implications of poor theory of mind for consultations?","What are the 3 implications of poor theory of mind for consultations?","Describing their own feelings to someone who doesn't know; Understanding roles and perspectives of people present; Theory of mind is an active process"],["","Why does a child without theory of mind say \"the box\"?","Why does a child without theory of mind say \"the box\"?","They know it's there; Don't realise Sally's knowledge differs"]]},{"id":12123,"t":"Children's Understanding of Illness","s":"A doctor asks three children why people get colds. Child A, aged 5, says \"I caught it from my friend because I sat next to her.\" Child B, aged 8, says \"I got sick because I was naughty and touched someone ill.\" Child C, aged 10, says \"The cold got inside me because I went out without a coat.\"<br><br>Which Bibace and Walsh stages are these?","o":["A: contagion; B: contamination; C: internalisation","A: phenomenism; B: contagion; C: physiological","A: contamination; B: internalisation; C: psychophysiological","A: contagion; B: physiological; C: contamination","A: internalisation; B: contagion; C: phenomenism, because a particular object caused the illness"],"e":"Step 1: at 4–7, contagion: illness is caused by proximity to ill people or objects (child A); at 7–9, contamination: illness is caused by physical contact and may be seen as punishment for misbehaviour (child B). Step 2: at 9–11, internalisation: illness is inside the body but may be caused by external factors such as being cold (child C).","w":["Phenomenism has no mechanism; child C does not describe organ malfunction.","Child A describes proximity (contagion), and psychophysiological is 16+.","Child B links illness to misbehaviour (contamination), not organs.","The stages are in the wrong order."],"k":"Contagion (4–7), contamination (7–9), internalisation (9–11).","rn":[["","What are the 6 Bibace & Walsh stages of explaining illness (in order)?","What are the 6 Bibace & Walsh stages of explaining illness (in order)?","Phenomenism (2-4); Contagion (4-7); Contamination (7-9); Internalisation (9-11); Physiological (11-16); Psychophysiological (16+); MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":12124,"t":"Vygotsky: Sociocultural Theory","s":"A 7-year-old with diabetes cannot use her glucose meter alone. When a nurse sits with her, gives prompts and demonstrates, she can complete the test herself. Over several visits, the nurse gradually gives less help as the girl gains confidence.<br><br>Which concepts describe the gap she can bridge with help, and the nurse's gradually reduced support?","o":["Zone of proximal development; scaffolding","Object permanence; egocentric speech","Conservation; reversibility","Secure base; theory of mind","Formal operations; deductive logic used to reason from the general principles of the meter"],"e":"Step 1: Vygotsky's zone of proximal development (ZPD) is the distance between what a child can do independently and what they can do with guidance or collaboration. Step 2: scaffolding is supported learning where existing skills and knowledge build confidence to progress, as the nurse reduces help over time.","w":["Object permanence and egocentric speech are not about guided learning.","These are Piagetian concrete operational skills.","A secure base is from attachment, and theory of mind is about others' beliefs.","Formal operations belong to Piaget's stage 4."],"k":"ZPD: gap between alone and with help; scaffolding = supported learning.","rn":[["","What 3 concepts come from Vygotsky's theory?","What 3 concepts come from Vygotsky's theory?","Zone of proximal development; Scaffolding; Language"],["","ZPD","ZPD","Zone of proximal development"]]},{"id":12125,"t":"Pain and Healthcare in Childhood","s":"A 4-year-old boy is in the emergency department after a fall. His mother says he is \"being dramatic\", but he says \"ow\" and holds his arm. The doctor wants the most valid measure of his pain, and later wants to use a structured scale suited to his age.<br><br>Which approach is best?","o":["Self-report is the gold standard; use a faces scale such as FACES","Rely on the mother's report, as children cannot report pain reliably","Use a numerical 0–10 scale, as it suits all ages","Wait until he is 7, as pain vocabulary only emerges at school age","Measure only heart rate, because pain is objective and children's own reports are subject to too many biases"],"e":"Step 1: pain is subjective, so self-report is the gold standard; children can report pain consistently and reliably, with basic pain vocabulary (\"ow\") from as early as 18 months. Step 2: face pain scales such as FACES (Wong and Baker, 1988) suit younger children, while numerical or visual analogue scales suit older children and adolescents.","w":["Children can report pain consistently and reliably.","Numerical scales are for older children and adolescents.","Basic pain vocabulary appears from about 18 months.","Pain is subjective; scales have biases, but self-report is still the gold standard."],"k":"Child pain: self-report gold standard; FACES for young children.","rn":[["","Why is self-report the gold standard for pain?","Why is self-report the gold standard for pain?","Pain is inherently subjective"]]},{"id":12126,"t":"Medical Consultations with Adolescents","s":"A 15-year-old girl attends her GP with her mother, complaining of tiredness. She answers briefly and avoids eye contact, and later says she felt everyone in the waiting room was watching her. Studies show many adolescents her age want to see the doctor alone, but few are given the chance.<br><br>Which concept explains her feeling, and what should the GP do?","o":["Imaginary audience; offer to see her alone and explain confidentiality","Personal fable; ask her mother to answer for her","Object permanence; give her a picture leaflet","Role confusion; postpone the consultation","Phenomenism; ask which particular object she thinks has caused her tiredness and treat that"],"e":"Step 1: Elkind's adolescent egocentrism includes the imaginary audience: feeling at the centre of attention and preoccupied with what others think, with embarrassment and a need for privacy. Step 2: adolescents are often dissatisfied with consultations because of privacy and confidentiality concerns; up to 52% wanted to see the doctor alone but only 20% were given the chance, so the GP should offer this and explain confidentiality.","w":["The personal fable is believing your feelings are unique, and the mother answering removes privacy.","Object permanence belongs to infancy.","Role confusion is Erikson's identity concept and is not a reason to postpone.","Phenomenism is a stage of 2–4-year-olds."],"k":"Imaginary audience → offer privacy and confidentiality to adolescents.","rn":[["","What are the 3 reasons adolescents are dissatisfied?","What are the 3 reasons adolescents are dissatisfied?","Privacy and confidentiality concerns; Embarrassment about sensitive issues; Not given chance to see doctor alone"]]},{"id":12127,"t":"Adolescent Brain Development","s":"A 17-year-old boy is brought in after crashing a car while racing his friends. He can describe the risks clearly when asked calmly, but says that in the moment, with his friends watching, he \"just did it\". His parents ask why he made such a poor decision.<br><br>Which explanation fits the lecture best?","o":["The prefrontal cortex, which controls inhibition and weighing consequences, matures into the 20s; peers are highly influential","The prefrontal cortex finishes developing at 12, so he chose to take the risk","Grey matter increases in adolescence, making decisions impulsive","He is in Piaget's concrete operational stage and cannot weigh consequences","Movement areas mature last, so he could not control the car"],"e":"Step 1: the prefrontal cortex develops into the 20s and is responsible for executive functions, including weighing consequences and inhibitory control. Step 2: although decision-making skills improve, adolescents are often good at making bad decisions (accidents are the leading cause of death), and friends are highly influential both positively and negatively.","w":["The prefrontal cortex continues developing into the 20s.","Grey matter decreases through pruning in adolescence.","At 17 he would be formal operational and can describe risks.","Areas for movement and senses mature first."],"k":"Prefrontal cortex (inhibition, consequences) matures into the 20s; peers influence risk.","rn":[["","What are the 6 executive functions of the prefrontal cortex?","What are the 6 executive functions of the prefrontal cortex?","Planning, organising thoughts; Problem solving; Weighing consequences; Assuming responsibility; Interpreting & controlling emotions; Inhibitory control"]]},{"id":12128,"t":"Children's Understanding of Illness","s":"A 6-year-old boy with cystic fibrosis has spent many weeks in hospital and has an older sister with the same condition. He explains his treatment clearly, including why he does his physiotherapy, using words most children his age would not know. A new doctor assumes he will understand very little because of his age.<br><br>What should the doctor take into account?","o":["Personal and family experience can give a child more understanding than expected for age","All children of 6 understand illness in the same way, so age is enough","Children with long-term conditions understand less than other children","Understanding depends only on Piaget's stage, not on experience","He must be in the psychophysiological stage, which normally starts at 6"],"e":"Step 1: a child's understanding of illness is affected by age and stage, language, culture, personal experience and family factors. Step 2: children with long-standing conditions (or siblings with them) may know much more than expected for their age, so the doctor should acknowledge wider experience, not just age or appearance.","w":["Understanding is shaped by experience as well as age.","Children with long-standing conditions may know more, not less.","Experience and family factors also matter.","The psychophysiological stage is 16+."],"k":"Experience (own or sibling's illness) can raise understanding beyond age.","rn":[["","What 5 factors does children's understanding of illness depend on?","What 5 factors does children's understanding of illness depend on?","Age and stage of development; Language / communication style and ability; Cultural background; Personal experience; Systemic and family factors"]]},{"id":12129,"t":"Adolescent Brain Development","s":"A research team scans the brains of a group of children every two years from age 8 to 20. Over this period, grey matter volume steadily falls while white matter increases. Connections that are used often become stronger, while rarely used ones disappear.<br><br>Which process explains these changes, and what is the result?","o":["Synaptic pruning, making connections more specialised and efficient","Neurodegeneration, causing loss of intellectual ability","Synaptic growth of grey matter, adding new connections","Hyperplasia of neurons in the prefrontal cortex","Adrenarche, which activates the adrenal axis and shrinks the grey matter of the cortex"],"e":"Step 1: in adolescence, synaptic pruning removes excess grey matter: frequently used connections are strengthened and infrequently used ones are eliminated, so grey matter decreases and white matter increases (Gogtay et al., 2004). Step 2: this reorganisation makes connections more specialised and efficient, with movement, vision and senses maturing first and memory and attention later.","w":["This is normal reorganisation, not loss of ability.","Grey matter decreases rather than grows.","The lecture does not describe neuron hyperplasia.","Adrenarche is a hormonal event of puberty, not the cause of pruning."],"k":"Pruning: grey matter ↓, white matter ↑ → more efficient connections.","rn":[["","Synaptic Pruning","Synaptic Pruning","Pruning of excess grey matter; Frequently used connections strengthened; Infrequently used connections eliminated"],["","Reorganisation (brain)","Reorganisation (brain)","Connections become more specialised and efficient"]]},{"id":12130,"t":"Puberty and Pubertal Timing","s":"A public health student compares menarche in Norway in 1840 and today. The mean age has fallen from 17 to 13, and in the UK it is now about 12.9. She also notes that in boys the growth spurt comes later than in girls, at around 14 rather than 12.<br><br>Which explanation for the fall does the lecture give, and how do boys' and girls' growth spurts differ?","o":["Better living standards, health and nutrition; girls' spurt comes before secondary sexual development, boys' after genital growth","Genetic change in the population; boys and girls have their spurt at the same age","More screen time; girls' spurt comes after boys'","Less body fat; the growth spurt occurs only in girls","Earlier schooling; boys' spurt comes before any genital growth, girls' only after breast development is complete"],"e":"Step 1: age at puberty has fallen over the last few hundred years, with causes including improved standards of living, health and nutrition, and body mass (girls ~17% body fat). Step 2: the linear growth spurt comes at about 12 in girls, before secondary sexual development, and at about 14 in boys, after the genitals have begun to grow.","w":["The lecture links the fall to living standards, not genetic change, and boys' spurt is later.","Girls' spurt comes before boys'.","Body mass (fat) is a cause, and both sexes have a spurt.","Boys' spurt comes after genital growth begins."],"k":"Earlier puberty: living standards, nutrition; girls' spurt ~12, boys' ~14.","rn":[["","Girls' growth spurt: before or after secondary sexual development?","Girls' growth spurt: before or after secondary sexual development?","Before (precedes it)"],["","What are the 3 causes of falling age at puberty?","What are the 3 causes of falling age at puberty?","Improved standard of living; Health and nutrition; Body mass"]]}]);
